window.PYQ_DATA.add({"id":"px-cbse12-acc-share-capital","kind":"practice","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"title":"Accounting for Share Capital · Practice","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","subject":"Accountancy","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","marking":{"mcq":[4,-1]},"rules":{},"note":"","questions":[{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-001","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":1,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"When a company forfeits shares of a defaulting shareholder, it means that the company:","image":"","options":["Refunds the amount already paid by the shareholder","Cancels the allotment and treats the amount already received on those shares as forfeited to the company","Transfers the shares to another member at face value","Treats the amount received as Calls in Advance"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT describes forfeiture as cancelling the allotment of a shareholder who fails to pay allotment or call money.\nThe amount already received on those shares is kept by the company as forfeited.\nThis is done under the company's Articles, usually based on Table F.\nNo refund is made, so option (1) is wrong.\nCalls in Advance means money paid before a call is made, which is unrelated to default, so option (4) is wrong.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-002","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":2,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The death or insolvency of a shareholder does not affect the existence of a company.\nReason (R): A company, being an artificial person created by law, enjoys perpetual succession and can be terminated only through law.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: members may come and go, but the company continues to exist.\nR is true: a company is an artificial person created by law. It has perpetual succession and can be terminated only through law.\nR directly explains why a member's death or insolvency cannot end the company.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-003","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":3,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"Sigma Ltd. made the first call of Rs. 3 per share on 5,000 equity shares of Rs. 10 each. Anil, holding 250 shares, failed to pay the first call. Bina, holding 300 shares, paid the first call and also the second and final call of Rs. 2 per share, which had not yet been made. The company records the receipt in one combined entry, using the Calls in Arrears and Calls in Advance accounts. With what amount will the Bank Account be debited in this entry?","image":"","options":["Rs. 15,000","Rs. 14,250","Rs. 15,600","Rs. 14,850"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Given: first call due = 5,000 × 3 = Rs. 15,000.\nCalls in arrears (Anil) = 250 × 3 = Rs. 750.\nCalls in advance (Bina) = 300 × 2 = Rs. 600.\nFormula: Bank = Call due − arrears + advance = 15,000 − 750 + 600 = Rs. 14,850.\nThe entry is: Bank Dr. 14,850 and Calls in Arrears Dr. 750, To First Call 15,000, To Calls in Advance 600.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-004","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":4,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"Unique Pictures Ltd. has 20,000, 5% preference shares of Rs. 10 each, fully paid up. The directors decide to pay the full preference dividend for the year. What is the amount of preference dividend payable?","image":"","options":["Rs. 1,000","Rs. 20,000","Rs. 5,000","Rs. 10,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Given: 20,000 preference shares of Rs. 10 each, with a dividend rate of 5%.\nFormula: Preference dividend = Number of shares × Nominal value × Rate. Under Section 43, the fixed rate is applied to the nominal value of the shares.\nSubstituting: 20,000 × Rs. 10 × 5% = Rs. 2,00,000 × 0.05.\nSo, preference dividend = Rs. 10,000.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-005","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":5,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"Under which head is the balance of Securities Premium Reserve Account shown in a company's balance sheet?","image":"","options":["Share Capital, under Shareholders' Funds","Reserves and Surplus, under Shareholders' Funds","Other Current Liabilities","Long-term Borrowings"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Premium received on issue of shares is credited to a separate Securities Premium Reserve Account, not to Share Capital Account.\nNCERT says this balance is shown under 'Equity and Liabilities', within 'Reserves and Surplus'.\nThe tempting option, Share Capital, is wrong. Share Capital holds only the nominal value of the shares, plus any share forfeiture balance.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-006","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":6,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"Kiran Ltd. issued 20,000 equity shares of Rs. 10 each at a premium of Rs. 3 per share. The shares were payable as follows:\nApplication: Rs. 4\nAllotment: Rs. 6 (including premium)\nFirst and final call: balance\nThe issue was fully subscribed. By what amount will Equity Share Capital Account be credited when allotment money falls due?","image":"","options":["Rs. 1,20,000","Rs. 60,000","Rs. 80,000","Rs. 40,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: allotment is Rs. 6 per share, of which Rs. 3 is premium.\nCapital part of allotment = 6 − 3 = Rs. 3 per share.\nEntry: Share Allotment A/c Dr. 1,20,000; To Share Capital A/c 60,000; To Securities Premium Reserve A/c 60,000.\nSo Share Capital is credited with 20,000 × 3 = Rs. 60,000.\nRs. 1,20,000 wrongly includes the premium.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-007","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":7,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"A company passes a special resolution that a part of its uncalled capital will be called up only if the company is wound up. This part of the capital is known as:","image":"","options":["Unissued capital","Reserve capital","Capital reserve","Calls in arrears"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Reserve capital is the portion of uncalled capital that can be called only when the company is wound up.\nIt is available only to the creditors at that time.\nCapital reserve sounds similar, but it is a reserve built from capital profits, such as profit on reissue of forfeited shares.\nUnissued capital is authorised capital that has not been offered to the public.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-008","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":8,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"As explained in the chapter, which of the following best describes a 'share'?","image":"","options":["A loan given by a person to the company carrying a fixed rate of interest","A fractional part of the share capital of a company that forms the basis of ownership interest in it","A certificate showing the amount of debentures held by a person","The maximum amount of capital a company is permitted to raise under its Memorandum"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Shares are the units into which the total share capital of a company is divided.\nEach share is a fractional part of that capital, and holding it gives an ownership interest in the company.\nA loan with fixed interest describes debentures (debt capital), not shares.\nThe maximum capital a company may raise is its authorised capital, not a share.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-009","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":9,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"Vihaan Ltd. offered 50,000 equity shares of Rs. 10 each to the public. Applications were received for 45,000 shares, which were all allotted. The company has called up Rs. 7 per share so far. Calls in arrears are Rs. 2 per share on 1,500 shares (first call). What is the paid-up capital of the company?","image":"","options":["Rs. 3,12,000","Rs. 3,15,000","Rs. 3,47,000","Rs. 4,47,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: 45,000 shares were subscribed and allotted, Rs. 7 per share has been called up, and calls in arrears are 1,500 × Rs. 2.\nFormula: Paid-up capital = Called-up capital − Calls in arrears.\nSubstituting: Called-up capital = 45,000 × 7 = Rs. 3,15,000. Calls in arrears = 1,500 × 2 = Rs. 3,000.\nSo paid-up capital = 3,15,000 − 3,000 = Rs. 3,12,000.\nOption (3) wrongly uses the 50,000 shares offered instead of the 45,000 subscribed. Option (2) ignores the calls in arrears.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-010","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":10,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): A company may open a single 'Share Application and Allotment Account' instead of separate application and allotment accounts.\nReason (R): Allotment without application is impossible, and application without allotment is meaningless, so the two stages are closely inter-related.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.","Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.","A is true, but R is false.","A is false, but R is true."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT states that companies sometimes maintain a combined 'Share Application and Allotment Account'.\nR is true. This is exactly the reasoning NCERT gives for the combined account: the application and allotment stages are inter-related.\nSince R is the reason the combined account is opened, R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-011","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":11,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) Calls in arrears represent the debit balance of the various call accounts.\n(ii) The balance of Calls in Advance is added to paid-up capital in the Notes to Accounts.\n(iii) Under Table F, interest on calls in advance can be paid at a rate not exceeding 12% p.a.\n(iv) When money is received as calls in advance, Calls in Advance A/c is debited.\n(v) Paid-up capital equals called-up capital minus calls in arrears.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: unpaid call money remains as a debit balance in the call accounts.\n(ii) Incorrect: calls in advance is a current liability and is not added to paid-up capital.\n(iii) Correct: Table F caps interest on calls in advance at 12% p.a.\n(iv) Incorrect: the entry is Bank Dr. To Calls in Advance, so the account is credited.\n(v) Correct: this is the NCERT definition of paid-up capital.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-012","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":12,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Amount received as calls in advance is not included in the paid-up share capital of a company.\nReason (R): Calls in advance is a liability of the company until the call to which it relates becomes due. The company may also have to pay interest on it, if the Articles so provide.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT states that the balance of Calls in Advance is shown separately under 'Other Current Liabilities' and is not added to paid-up capital.\nR is true. The amount is owed back to the shareholder as a liability until the relevant call is made. At that point it is adjusted by the entry: Calls in Advance Dr. To Call A/c. Interest may also be payable on it.\nR explains A: because the amount is a liability and not yet share capital, it cannot form part of paid-up capital.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-013","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":13,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"Arun Ltd. allotted 50,000 equity shares of Rs. 10 each, payable Rs. 2 on application, Rs. 3 on allotment, Rs. 3 on first call and Rs. 2 on final call. The final call has not yet been made. All amounts called were received except the first call on 1,500 shares. One shareholder holding 1,000 shares also paid the final call money in advance. At what amount will the subscribed capital be shown in the Notes to Accounts (net of calls in arrears)?","image":"","options":["Rs. 3,97,500","Rs. 4,00,000","Rs. 3,95,500","Rs. 3,93,500"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: Rs. 8 per share has been called on 50,000 shares. The first call of Rs. 3 is unpaid on 1,500 shares, and calls in advance are Rs. 2 on 1,000 shares.\nFormula: Paid-up capital = Called-up capital − Calls in arrears. Calls in advance is a current liability and is not added.\nSubstituting: Called-up = 50,000 × 8 = Rs. 4,00,000. Arrears = 1,500 × 3 = Rs. 4,500.\nSo the amount shown = 4,00,000 − 4,500 = Rs. 3,95,500.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-014","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":14,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"Chief Justice Marshall described a company as a person who exists only in the eyes of law. Which set of words did he use to describe this person?","image":"","options":["Natural, visible and tangible","Artificial, invisible and intangible","Artificial, visible and tangible","Natural, invisible and intangible"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Chief Justice Marshall called a company \"a person, artificial, invisible, intangible and existing only in the eyes of law\".\nIt is artificial because law creates it, not birth.\nIt is invisible and intangible because it cannot be seen or touched; only its acts through directors and documents can be seen.\nOptions using 'natural', 'visible' or 'tangible' contradict the definition.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-015","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":15,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"Nova Ltd. purchased machinery worth Rs. 4,80,000 from Arora Engineering. Nova Ltd. paid the full price by issuing equity shares of Rs. 10 each at a premium of 20%. Find the number of shares issued.","image":"","options":["48,000","57,600","38,400","40,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Given: Amount payable = Rs. 4,80,000; face value = Rs. 10; premium = 20% = Rs. 2.\nIssue price = 10 + 2 = Rs. 12.\nFormula: Number of shares = Amount payable ÷ Issue price.\nSubstituting: 4,80,000 ÷ 12 = 40,000 shares.\n48,000 wrongly uses the face value as the issue price.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-016","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":16,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"Neha held 400 equity shares of Rs. 10 each, issued at a premium of Rs. 3 per share. The shares were payable as Rs. 3 on application, Rs. 5 on allotment (including the premium of Rs. 3), and Rs. 5 on first and final call. She paid only the application money, and her shares were forfeited after the call. Which journal entry correctly records the forfeiture?","image":"","options":["Share Capital A/c Dr. 4,000; Securities Premium Reserve A/c Dr. 1,200; To Share Allotment A/c 2,000; To Share First and Final Call A/c 2,000; To Share Forfeiture A/c 1,200","Share Capital A/c Dr. 5,200; To Share Allotment A/c 2,000; To Share First and Final Call A/c 2,000; To Share Forfeiture A/c 1,200","Share Capital A/c Dr. 4,000; Securities Premium Reserve A/c Dr. 1,200; To Share Allotment A/c 2,000; To Share First and Final Call A/c 2,000; To Capital Reserve A/c 1,200","Share Capital A/c Dr. 4,000; To Share Allotment A/c 800; To Share First and Final Call A/c 2,000; To Share Forfeiture A/c 1,200"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Step 1: Debit Share Capital with the called-up amount: 400 × 10 = Rs. 4,000.\nStep 2: The premium was due on allotment and was not received. So debit Securities Premium Reserve with 400 × 3 = Rs. 1,200.\nStep 3: Credit the unpaid amounts. Allotment: 400 × 5 = Rs. 2,000. Call: 400 × 5 = Rs. 2,000.\nStep 4: Credit Share Forfeiture with the amount received: 400 × 3 = Rs. 1,200.\nCapital Reserve is credited only after reissue, so option (3) is wrong.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-017","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":17,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Under Table F, a company may call Rs. 20 per share from some equity shareholders and Rs. 10 per share from other equity shareholders on the same call.\nStatement II: Under Table F, shareholders must be given a minimum of 14 days' notice to pay the call amount.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Statement I is incorrect. Calls must be made on a uniform basis on all shares within the same class, so different equity shareholders cannot be called for different amounts on the same call.\nStatement II is correct. Table F requires a minimum of 14 days' notice to shareholders for paying the call amount.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-018","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":18,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"A major shareholder of Arvind Textiles Ltd. dies and another becomes insolvent in the same year. The company continues its business without interruption. Which feature of a company does this show?","image":"","options":["Limited liability","Common seal","Perpetual succession","Transferability of shares"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Perpetual succession means the company keeps existing whatever changes occur in its membership.\nThe death, insanity or insolvency of a member does not affect the company. Members may come and go, but the company continues. It can be ended only through law.\nLimited liability concerns how much a member must pay, so it does not explain why the company survives.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-019","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":19,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: When a shareholder pays calls in arrears together with interest, the interest is credited to an interest account and the call money is credited to the Calls in Arrears Account.\nStatement II: If the Articles are silent, Table F permits interest on calls in advance at a rate not exceeding 10% per annum.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. The entry is: Bank A/c Dr., To Calls in Arrears A/c, To Interest on Calls in Arrears A/c.\nStatement II is incorrect. Table F allows interest on calls in advance at up to 12% p.a. The 10% ceiling applies to calls in arrears.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-020","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":20,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"Directors of Sagar Ltd. forfeited 400 equity shares of Rs. 100 each for non-payment of the second and final call of Rs. 25 per share. Of these, 300 shares were reissued at Rs. 90 per share as fully paid. Calculate the amount to be transferred to Capital Reserve.","image":"","options":["Rs. 19,500","Rs. 27,000","Rs. 22,500","Rs. 26,500"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: Rs. 75 was received per share, so total forfeiture = 400 × 75 = Rs. 30,000.\nForfeiture relating to the 300 reissued shares = 300 × 75 = Rs. 22,500.\nDiscount on reissue = 300 × (100 − 90) = Rs. 3,000, debited to Share Forfeiture A/c.\nCapital Reserve = 22,500 − 3,000 = Rs. 19,500. The Rs. 7,500 on the 100 unissued shares stays in the forfeiture account.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-021","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":21,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"Read the following statements about over-subscription of shares:\nStatement I: Over-subscription usually happens when well-managed and financially strong companies issue shares.\nStatement II: When shares are allotted pro-rata, the excess application money must always be refunded to the applicants.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. NCERT notes that over-subscription is usually seen when well-managed, financially strong companies issue shares.\nStatement II is incorrect. Under pro-rata allotment, the excess application money is normally adjusted towards the amount due on allotment.\nOnly the part that is more than the allotment money is refunded or credited to Calls in Advance.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-022","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":22,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"In which document is the maximum amount of share capital that a company may raise stated, and what is this capital also called?","image":"","options":["Articles of Association; Issued capital","Memorandum of Association; Nominal or Registered capital","Prospectus; Subscribed capital","Memorandum of Association; Called-up capital"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Authorised capital is the amount of share capital a company is authorised to issue by its Memorandum of Association. The company cannot raise more than this amount.\nIt is also called Nominal or Registered capital, so option (2) fits.\nThe Articles of Association set out the classes of shares and their rights, not the ceiling on capital, so option (1) fails.\nA prospectus only invites the public to subscribe. Called-up capital is the part of subscribed capital that the company has demanded.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-023","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":23,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (Transaction) with List-II (Journal entry):\nList-I\nA. Application money received\nB. Amount due on allotment\nC. Money refunded on rejected applications\nD. First call money received\nList-II\n(i) Share Application A/c Dr. To Bank A/c\n(ii) Bank A/c Dr. To Share First Call A/c\n(iii) Bank A/c Dr. To Share Application A/c\n(iv) Share Allotment A/c Dr. To Share Capital A/c","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(i), B-(iv), C-(iii), D-(ii)","A-(iii), B-(ii), C-(i), D-(iv)","A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(ii), D-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A-(iii): Bank receives the cash, so Bank is debited and Share Application is credited.\nB-(iv): The amount due is debited to Share Allotment A/c and credited to Share Capital A/c.\nC-(i): The refund reduces the application balance, so Share Application is debited and Bank is credited.\nD-(ii): Cash is received against the first call, so Bank is debited and Share First Call is credited.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-024","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":24,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"Pearl Ltd. issued 10,000 equity shares of Rs. 10 each. The second and final call was Rs. 2.50 per share. All shareholders paid except Meena, who holds 200 shares. The company maintains a Calls in Arrears Account. With what amount will the Calls in Arrears Account be debited when the final call money is received?","image":"","options":["Rs. 250","Rs. 500","Rs. 2,000","Rs. 24,500"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: 200 shares unpaid, final call of Rs. 2.50 per share.\nFormula: Calls in arrears = unpaid shares × call per share.\nSubstituting: 200 × 2.50 = Rs. 500.\nThe entry is: Bank A/c Dr. 24,500 and Calls in Arrears A/c Dr. 500, To Share Second and Final Call A/c 25,000.\nSo the amount debited is Rs. 500.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-025","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":25,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"Omega Ltd. issued 40,000 equity shares of Rs. 10 each at par, payable as Rs. 3 on application, Rs. 4 on allotment and Rs. 3 on first and final call. Applications were received for 50,000 shares. All applicants were allotted shares pro-rata, and the excess application money was adjusted towards allotment. If all allotment money due was received, how much cash did the company receive on allotment?","image":"","options":["Rs. 1,60,000","Rs. 1,90,000","Rs. 1,30,000","Rs. 1,00,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: 40,000 shares allotted, Rs. 4 per share due on allotment, and applications for 50,000 shares at Rs. 3 each.\nExcess application money = (50,000 − 40,000) × Rs. 3 = Rs. 30,000.\nAllotment money due = 40,000 × Rs. 4 = Rs. 1,60,000.\nCash received on allotment = 1,60,000 − 30,000 = Rs. 1,30,000.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-026","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":26,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"Case: Zeta Ltd. forfeited shares of Rs. 10 each held by two shareholders:\n- Amit: 200 shares on which Rs. 5 per share had been received.\n- Bela: 300 shares on which Rs. 8 per share had been received.\nThe company reissued 400 forfeited shares at Rs. 7 per share as fully paid. These included all of Bela's shares and 100 of Amit's shares.\nWhat amount should be transferred to Capital Reserve?","image":"","options":["Rs. 1,700","Rs. 2,200","Rs. 2,900","Rs. 500"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: forfeiture on reissued shares = Bela 300 × 8 + Amit 100 × 5 = 2,400 + 500 = Rs. 2,900.\nDiscount on reissue = 400 × (10 − 7) = Rs. 1,200, debited to Share Forfeiture A/c. This is within the limit, since Rs. 3 ≤ Rs. 5 for Amit's shares.\nCapital Reserve = 2,900 − 1,200 = Rs. 1,700.\nAmit's 100 unissued shares keep Rs. 500 in Share Forfeiture A/c. Rs. 2,200 wrongly uses the total forfeiture of Rs. 3,400.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-027","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":27,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about the share issue procedure are correct?\n(i) Application money is deposited with a scheduled bank specified in the prospectus.\n(ii) Letters of regret are sent to applicants to whom no shares are allotted.\n(iii) Allotment of shares results in a valid contract between the company and the applicants.\n(iv) If minimum subscription is not received in time, application money must be returned within 130 days of the date of issue of the prospectus.\n(v) The prospectus is issued to the public only after the allotment of shares.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: application money goes to the scheduled bank named in the prospectus.\n(ii) Correct: applicants who get no shares receive letters of regret; allottees receive letters of allotment.\n(iii) Correct: on allotment, applicants become shareholders under a valid contract.\n(iv) Correct: the refund must be made within 130 days of issue of the prospectus.\n(v) Incorrect: the prospectus is the first step, an invitation to the public issued before applications and allotment.\nSo 4 statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-028","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":28,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"A company forfeited 300 equity shares of Rs. 10 each for non-payment of the final call of Rs. 4 per share; Rs. 6 per share had been received on them. Out of these, 200 shares were reissued as fully paid at Rs. 8 per share. What amount will be transferred to Capital Reserve?","image":"","options":["Rs. 1,400","Rs. 1,200","Rs. 800","Rs. 400"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: amount forfeited per share = Rs. 6. Shares reissued = 200. Discount on reissue = 10 − 8 = Rs. 2 per share.\nFormula: Capital Reserve = Forfeited amount on reissued shares − Discount on reissue.\nSubstituting: forfeited amount on 200 shares = 200 × 6 = Rs. 1,200. Discount = 200 × 2 = Rs. 400.\nSo Capital Reserve = 1,200 − 400 = Rs. 800.\nThe Rs. 600 on the 100 shares not reissued stays in Share Forfeiture Account.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-029","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":29,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"As per NCERT, a company can issue shares at a discount only in which of the following cases?","image":"","options":["Fresh public issue and rights issue","Reissue of forfeited shares and issue of sweat equity shares","Issue of shares to vendors and private placement","Issue of preference shares and bonus shares"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"As a general rule, a company cannot ordinarily issue shares at a discount, meaning below nominal value.\nNCERT allows exactly two exceptions: reissue of forfeited shares and issue of sweat equity shares.\nShares to vendors may be issued at par or at a premium. A fresh public issue cannot be made at a discount.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-030","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":30,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"Sigma Ltd. issued 5,000 equity shares of Rs. 100 each on these terms:\n- Rs. 25 on application\n- Rs. 30 on allotment\n- Rs. 20 on first call\n- The balance of Rs. 25 to be called as and when required\n\nAll money up to allotment was received. When the first call was made:\n- A shareholder holding 200 shares failed to pay the call.\n- Another shareholder holding 100 shares paid the entire remaining amount on his shares.\n\nThe company recorded the receipt in one combined journal entry. What amount will be debited to Bank A/c in that entry?","image":"","options":["Rs. 98,500","Rs. 96,000","Rs. 1,02,500","Rs. 94,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: First call due = 5,000 × Rs. 20 = Rs. 1,00,000.\nCalls in arrears = 200 × Rs. 20 = Rs. 4,000.\nCalls in advance = 100 × Rs. 25 = Rs. 2,500 (the uncalled balance).\nFormula: Bank = Call due − Arrears + Advance.\nSubstituting: 1,00,000 − 4,000 + 2,500 = Rs. 98,500.\nThe combined entry is: Bank Dr. 98,500 and Calls in Arrears Dr. 4,000; To First Call 1,00,000 and To Calls in Advance 2,500.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-031","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":31,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["Dividend on preference shares must be paid before any dividend is paid to equity shareholders.","Equity shareholders bear the residual risk, as they receive profits only after the claims of preference shareholders are met.","The right of preference shareholders to participate in surpluses may be specified in the Memorandum or Articles of the company.","The amount of authorised capital, as well as the classes of shares with their respective rights and obligations, are prescribed in the Memorandum of Association."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: preference dividend has priority over equity dividend.\n(2) Correct: equity shareholders get only what remains after the preference dividend, so they bear the residual risk.\n(3) Correct: the participation right of preference shareholders is specified in the Memorandum or Articles.\n(4) Incorrect: the Memorandum states the authorised capital and the number of shares into which it is divided. The classes of shares, with their rights and obligations, are prescribed by the Articles of Association.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-032","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":32,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The balance in Share Forfeiture Account relating to forfeited shares not yet reissued is shown as an addition to the paid-up capital under 'Share Capital'.\nReason (R): The gain on reissue of forfeited shares is transferred to Capital Reserve.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A is true: until the forfeited shares are reissued, the share forfeiture balance is added to paid-up capital under Share Capital.\nR is true: after reissue, the remaining balance on the reissued shares is a capital profit and goes to Capital Reserve.\nHowever, R deals with shares already reissued. A deals with shares not yet reissued. So R does not explain A.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-033","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":33,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"Ravi holds 400 shares of Kiran Ltd. He did not pay the first call of Rs. 3 per share, which was due on 1 August 2024. He paid the call money on 31 January 2025, along with interest. The Articles provide for interest on calls in arrears at 10% p.a. What amount of interest will be credited to the Interest on Calls in Arrears Account?","image":"","options":["Rs. 60","Rs. 72","Rs. 120","Rs. 30"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: arrears = 400 × Rs. 3 = Rs. 1,200; rate = 10% p.a.; period from 1 August to 31 January = 6 months.\nFormula: Interest = Arrears × Rate × Time.\nSubstituting: 1,200 × 10/100 × 6/12 = Rs. 60.\nThe entry is: Bank A/c Dr. 1,260, To Calls in Arrears A/c 1,200, To Interest on Calls in Arrears A/c 60.\nRs. 72 wrongly uses 12% p.a.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-034","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":34,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"Case: Nova Ltd. offered 2,00,000 equity shares of Rs. 10 each to the public, with Rs. 3 payable on application. It received applications for 1,75,000 shares within 120 days:\n- applications for 75,000 shares were made through ASBA;\n- applications for 1,00,000 shares were made with application money paid by cheque.\nSince minimum subscription was not received, no allotment was made. What amount must Nova Ltd. refund from its own bank account?","image":"","options":["Rs. 3,00,000","Rs. 5,25,000","Rs. 2,25,000","Nil"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Step 1 – check minimum subscription: 90% × 2,00,000 = 1,80,000 shares. Only 1,75,000 shares were applied for, so the issue fails and no shares are allotted.\nStep 2 – ASBA applications: the bank only blocked the money and debits it only on allotment. With no allotment, the bank simply removes the lien, so the company never received this money.\nStep 3 – cheque applications: the company received 1,00,000 × Rs. 3 = Rs. 3,00,000, and this must be refunded.\nSo the refund from the company's bank account = Rs. 3,00,000.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-035","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":35,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: When a company maintains a combined Share Application and Allotment Account, the balance of allotment money received is credited to the Share Application and Allotment Account.\nStatement II: Even under the combined account method, a separate Share Allotment Account is debited for the amount due on allotment.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. The entry is Bank A/c Dr. To Share Application and Allotment A/c for the balance of allotment money received.\nStatement II is incorrect. Under the combined method, no separate allotment account exists.\nThe combined account itself is debited, and Share Capital A/c is credited, for the application money and the allotment amount due.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-036","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":36,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II:\nList-I\n(A) Authorised capital\n(B) Reserve capital\n(C) Uncalled capital\n(D) Paid-up capital\nList-II\n(i) Called-up capital minus calls in arrears\n(ii) Maximum capital the company may issue as per its Memorandum of Association\n(iii) Part of uncalled capital to be called only on winding up\n(iv) Part of subscribed capital not yet called up","image":"","options":["A-(ii), B-(iii), C-(iv), D-(i)","A-(ii), B-(iv), C-(iii), D-(i)","A-(i), B-(iii), C-(iv), D-(ii)","A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(ii), D-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Authorised (nominal/registered) capital is the maximum capital fixed by the Memorandum, so A matches (ii).\n(B) Reserve capital is the uncalled amount kept aside to be called only on winding up, so B matches (iii).\n(C) Uncalled capital is the subscribed capital not yet called, so C matches (iv).\n(D) Paid-up capital equals called-up capital less calls in arrears, so D matches (i).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-037","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":37,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"Arunodaya Ltd. is registered with a capital of Rs. 1,00,00,000 divided into 10,00,000 equity shares of Rs. 10 each. It issued 6,00,000 shares to the public, of which 5,80,000 shares were subscribed and allotted. Rs. 8 per share has been called up. Calls in arrears are Rs. 3 per share on 4,000 shares. By special resolution, Rs. 1 per share of the uncalled amount is to be called only on winding up.\nMatch List-I with List-II:\nList-I\n(A) Unissued capital\n(B) Uncalled capital\n(C) Paid-up capital\n(D) Reserve capital\nList-II\n(i) Rs. 5,80,000\n(ii) Rs. 11,60,000\n(iii) Rs. 40,00,000\n(iv) Rs. 46,28,000","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(ii), C-(iv), D-(i)","A-(iii), B-(i), C-(iv), D-(ii)","A-(ii), B-(iii), C-(i), D-(iv)","A-(iv), B-(ii), C-(iii), D-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Unissued capital = (10,00,000 − 6,00,000) × 10 = Rs. 40,00,000, which matches (iii).\n(B) Uncalled capital = 5,80,000 subscribed shares × (10 − 8) = Rs. 11,60,000, which matches (ii).\n(C) Paid-up capital = called-up capital − calls in arrears = (5,80,000 × 8) − (4,000 × 3) = 46,40,000 − 12,000 = Rs. 46,28,000, which matches (iv).\n(D) Reserve capital = 5,80,000 × Rs. 1 = Rs. 5,80,000, which matches (i). Reserve capital is part of the uncalled capital and is available to creditors only on winding up.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-038","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":38,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about the issue of shares are correct, as per the chapter?\n(i) Application money should be at least 5% of the face value of the share.\n(ii) Under Table A, the amount of a call should not exceed 25% of the face value of the share.\n(iii) Under Table A, at least one month must elapse between two calls.\n(iv) A minimum of 14 days' notice must be given to shareholders to pay a call.\n(v) Minimum subscription cannot be less than 90% of the issued amount.\n(vi) Minimum subscription must be received within 130 days from the date of issue of the prospectus.\n(vii) Calls may be made at different rates on different shares of the same class.","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: application money must be at least 5% of face value.\n(ii) Correct: a call cannot exceed 25% of face value.\n(iii) Correct: a one-month gap is required between calls.\n(iv) Correct: 14 days' notice must be given.\n(v) Correct: this follows the SEBI guideline.\n(vi) Incorrect: the limit is 120 days, and 130 days is the deadline for refunding application money.\n(vii) Incorrect: calls must be made on a uniform basis within a class.\nSo 5 statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-039","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":39,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (situation) with List-II (accounts credited when Share Application A/c is debited to close it):\nList-I\n(A) Some applications accepted in full and the others rejected\n(B) Pro-rata allotment to all applicants, with excess money less than the allotment due\n(C) Some applications rejected outright and pro-rata allotment to the rest\n(D) Under-subscription with minimum subscription received\nList-II\n(I) Share Capital A/c only\n(II) Share Capital A/c and Bank A/c\n(III) Share Capital A/c and Share Allotment A/c\n(IV) Share Capital A/c, Share Allotment A/c and Bank A/c","image":"","options":["A-II, B-III, C-IV, D-I","A-III, B-II, C-IV, D-I","A-II, B-IV, C-III, D-I","A-I, B-III, C-IV, D-II"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) First alternative: money on allotted shares goes to Share Capital and money on rejected applications is refunded through Bank. So A-II.\n(B) Pro-rata to all: excess money is credited to Share Allotment, and nothing is refunded. So B-III.\n(C) Combination: excess money goes to Share Allotment and money on rejected applications is refunded through Bank. So C-IV.\n(D) Under-subscription: every applicant is allotted, so all application money goes to Share Capital. So D-I.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-040","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":40,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"A company invited applications for 50,000 shares of Rs. 10 each, payable Rs. 3 on application, Rs. 4 on allotment and Rs. 3 on first and final call. Applications were received for 75,000 shares. Pro-rata allotment was made to all applicants, and excess application money was adjusted towards allotment. Ravi, who was allotted 1,200 shares, failed to pay the allotment and the call money. His shares were forfeited after the call. What amount will be credited to Share Forfeiture A/c?","image":"","options":["Rs. 3,600","Rs. 5,400","Rs. 7,200","Rs. 1,800"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Pro-rata ratio = 50,000 : 75,000 = 2 : 3, so Ravi applied for 1,200 × 3/2 = 1,800 shares.\nAmount paid by Ravi = 1,800 × Rs. 3 = Rs. 5,400. This includes the excess of Rs. 1,800 adjusted to allotment.\nUnpaid allotment = 1,200 × Rs. 4 − Rs. 1,800 = Rs. 3,000. Unpaid call = 1,200 × Rs. 3 = Rs. 3,600.\nEntry: Share Capital Dr. Rs. 12,000; To Allotment Rs. 3,000; To Call Rs. 3,600; To Share Forfeiture Rs. 5,400.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-041","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":41,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"Pristine Ltd. bought a building from Ganga Ltd. for Rs. 7,50,000. It paid Rs. 50,000 by cheque and the balance by issuing equity shares of Rs. 100 each at a premium of 25%. How many shares did Pristine Ltd. issue to Ganga Ltd.?","image":"","options":["6,000","7,000","5,600","7,500"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: Price = Rs. 7,50,000; cash paid = Rs. 50,000.\nAmount payable in shares = 7,50,000 − 50,000 = Rs. 7,00,000.\nIssue price = 100 + 25 = Rs. 125.\nNumber of shares = 7,00,000 ÷ 125 = 5,600.\n6,000 ignores the cash payment, and 7,000 ignores the premium.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-042","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":42,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"A company issued equity shares of Rs. 10 each at a premium of Rs. 2. The shares were payable Rs. 3 on application, Rs. 5 on allotment (including premium) and Rs. 4 on first and final call. Kavya, holding 200 shares, paid only the application money. Her shares were forfeited after the final call. Match the accounts in the forfeiture entry (List-I) with their amounts (List-II).\nList-I\n(A) Share Capital A/c (Dr.)\n(B) Securities Premium Reserve A/c (Dr.)\n(C) Share Allotment A/c (Cr.)\n(D) Share Forfeiture A/c (Cr.)\nList-II\n(i) Rs. 600\n(ii) Rs. 1,000\n(iii) Rs. 400\n(iv) Rs. 2,000","image":"","options":["A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(ii), D-(i)","A-(iv), B-(i), C-(ii), D-(iii)","A-(ii), B-(iii), C-(iv), D-(i)","A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(i), D-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Share Capital Dr. = called up 200 × Rs. 10 = Rs. 2,000, which is (iv).\n(B) Premium not received: 200 × Rs. 2 = Rs. 400, which is (iii).\n(C) Unpaid allotment: 200 × Rs. 5 = Rs. 1,000, which is (ii).\n(D) Amount received: 200 × Rs. 3 = Rs. 600, which is (i).\nThe final call of Rs. 800 is also credited. Check: debits Rs. 2,400 = credits Rs. 1,000 + Rs. 800 + Rs. 600.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-043","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":43,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The balance of Calls in Advance Account is not added to the paid-up capital. It is shown separately under 'Other Current Liabilities' in the balance sheet.\nReason (R): Money received before a call is made is a liability of the company until the call becomes due.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT says the calls in advance balance appears under Current Liabilities, sub-head Other Current Liabilities. It is not added to paid-up capital.\nR is true. The amount has not yet been called, so the company owes it to the shareholder. It may even pay interest on it (up to 12% p.a. under Table F).\nR explains A: the item is shown as a liability precisely because it is a liability and not capital.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-044","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":44,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"The Articles of Association of Zenith Ltd. say nothing about interest on unpaid calls. According to Table F, what is the maximum rate of interest the company can charge on calls in arrears?","image":"","options":["6% p.a.","10% p.a.","12% p.a.","15% p.a."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"When the Articles are silent, Table F applies.\nTable F allows interest of not more than 10% p.a. on unpaid amounts. Interest runs from the day fixed for payment to the day of actual payment.\n12% p.a. is the Table F ceiling for interest on calls in advance, not calls in arrears. That is the tempting trap.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-045","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":45,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"Arora Ltd. offered 15,000 equity shares of Rs. 10 each, with Rs. 4 payable on application. It received applications for 18,000 shares. Applications for 3,000 shares were rejected and their money was refunded in full. What amount is credited to Bank A/c for the refund?","image":"","options":["Rs. 72,000","Rs. 60,000","Rs. 30,000","Rs. 12,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Given: 3,000 shares were rejected; application money is Rs. 4 per share.\nFormula: Refund = Rejected shares × Application money per share.\nSubstituting: 3,000 × 4 = Rs. 12,000.\nEntry: Share Application A/c Dr. To Bank A/c Rs. 12,000.\nRs. 72,000 is the total application money received, and Rs. 60,000 is the amount transferred to share capital.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-046","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":46,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"Case: Orbit Ltd. invited applications for 40,000 equity shares of Rs. 10 each. The shares were payable Rs. 3 on application, Rs. 4 on allotment and Rs. 3 on first and final call. Applications were received for 60,000 shares, and a pro-rata allotment was made to all applicants. Excess application money was adjusted towards allotment. Ram, who was allotted 600 shares, failed to pay the allotment money, and his shares were forfeited immediately after allotment. With what amount will Share Forfeiture Account be credited?","image":"","options":["Rs. 2,700","Rs. 1,800","Rs. 1,500","Rs. 900"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: Pro-rata ratio = 40,000 : 60,000 = 2 : 3, so Ram applied for 600 × 3/2 = 900 shares.\nApplication money paid = 900 × 3 = Rs. 2,700. Of this, Rs. 1,800 went to capital and Rs. 900 was adjusted against allotment.\nAllotment due from Ram = 600 × 4 − 900 = Rs. 1,500.\nForfeiture entry: Share Capital Dr. 600 × 7 = Rs. 4,200. Credit Allotment Rs. 1,500 and Share Forfeiture Rs. 2,700, the total amount received from him.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-047","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":47,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following correctly describes the dividend on equity shares?","image":"","options":["It is fixed at the time of issue and is paid before the preference dividend","It is not fixed, may vary from year to year with available profits, and is paid only after the preference dividend","It is a fixed percentage of the market value of the shares, paid every year","It is paid only on the winding up of the company"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Equity shareholders share the distributable profits only after the dividend rights of preference shareholders have been satisfied.\nThe equity dividend is not fixed. It may vary from year to year depending on the profits available for distribution.\nOption (1) fails on two counts: the equity dividend is not fixed, and it comes after the preference dividend, not before.\nDividend has no connection with winding up, so option (4) is also wrong.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-048","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":48,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Some companies open a single 'Share Application and Allotment Account' instead of separate application and allotment accounts.\nReason (R): Allotment without application is impossible, while application without allotment is meaningless.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: NCERT states that a combined Share Application and Allotment Account is sometimes opened.\nR is true: this is exactly the reasoning NCERT gives, because the two stages are closely inter-related.\nSince R is the reason for keeping a combined account, R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-049","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":49,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): After forfeited shares are reissued, the balance left in the Share Forfeiture Account relating to those shares is transferred to Capital Reserve.\nReason (R): The surplus on reissue of forfeited shares is a capital profit and not a revenue profit.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A – True: NCERT states that the balance left in the forfeiture account relating to reissued shares is transferred to Capital Reserve.\nR – True: this surplus arises from a capital transaction, so it is a capital profit.\nCapital profits go to Capital Reserve. R therefore correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-050","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":50,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Unlimited companies are not found in India.\nReason (R): The Companies Act does not permit the formation of unlimited companies in India.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT states that unlimited companies are not found in India.\nR is false. NCERT says such companies are not found in India even though the Companies Act permits them.\nIn an unlimited company, members' private property can be used to pay company debts. That risk may explain why investors avoid them, but the law does not prohibit them.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-051","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":51,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"Tara Ltd. forfeited 300 equity shares of Rs. 10 each, issued at par, for non-payment of the final call of Rs. 3 per share. All earlier money had been received. Out of these, 200 shares were reissued at Rs. 8 per share as fully paid. What amount will be transferred to Capital Reserve?","image":"","options":["Rs. 1,000","Rs. 1,400","Rs. 1,700","Rs. 2,100"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: Amount forfeited = 300 × (10 − 3) = Rs. 2,100.\nFormula: Capital Reserve = forfeited amount on reissued shares − discount on reissue.\nForfeited amount on the 200 reissued shares = 200 × 7 = Rs. 1,400.\nDiscount on reissue = 200 × 2 = Rs. 400.\nSubstituting: 1,400 − 400 = Rs. 1,000.\nRs. 1,700 is wrong because it uses the whole forfeiture balance, including the 100 shares not yet reissued.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-052","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":52,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"A well-known company receives applications for more shares than it has offered to the public. According to NCERT, which course of action do directors most commonly adopt in practice?","image":"","options":["Accept some applications in full and reject the remaining ones outright","Make a pro-rata allotment to every applicant","Reject some applications outright and make a pro-rata allotment to the rest","Allot shares to every applicant in full by increasing the issued capital"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT lists three alternatives for over-subscription: (1) full acceptance of some applications and rejection of others, (2) pro-rata allotment to all, and (3) a combination of the two.\nNCERT states that the combination is the course most commonly adopted in practice.\nOptions (1) and (2) are valid alternatives, but neither is described as the most common one.\nOption (4) is wrong because allotment can never exceed the number of shares offered.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-053","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":53,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"When shares are allotted, the application money received on those shares is moved to the Share Capital Account. Which journal entry records this?","image":"","options":["Bank A/c Dr. To Share Application A/c","Share Application A/c Dr. To Share Capital A/c","Share Capital A/c Dr. To Share Application A/c","Share Allotment A/c Dr. To Share Capital A/c"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Once shares are allotted, the application money on them formally becomes part of share capital.\nThe temporary Share Application A/c is debited to close it, and Share Capital A/c is credited.\nOption (1) records the receipt of application money, which happens earlier.\nOption (4) records the amount due on allotment, not the transfer of application money.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-054","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":54,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In a company limited by guarantee, the liability of members arises only in the event of the company being wound up.\nStatement II: A private company limits the number of its members to 200, including its employees.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Members of a company limited by guarantee promise to contribute only if the company is wound up, so their liability arises only then.\nStatement II is incorrect. The limit of 200 members for a private company excludes its employees, not includes them.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-055","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":55,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"When forfeited shares are reissued as fully paid at a discount, the amount of discount allowed is debited to:","image":"","options":["Securities Premium Reserve A/c","Capital Reserve A/c","Share Forfeiture A/c","Statement of Profit and Loss"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT rule: the discount allowed on reissue of forfeited shares is debited to the Share Forfeiture (Forfeited Shares) Account.\nThe entry is: Bank A/c Dr., Share Forfeiture A/c Dr., To Share Capital A/c.\nCapital Reserve is the tempting option, but it is credited later. It receives only the surplus left in Share Forfeiture A/c after the discount is adjusted. It is never debited with the discount.\nSecurities Premium and the Statement of Profit and Loss are not used for this discount.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-056","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":56,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"Sigma Ltd. offered 50,000 equity shares of Rs. 10 each to the public. It received applications for only 47,000 shares. The minimum subscription of 90% of the issue was met. How many shares will the company allot?","image":"","options":["50,000 shares","45,000 shares","47,000 shares","3,000 shares"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: shares offered = 50,000 and shares applied for = 47,000.\nMinimum subscription required = 90% × 50,000 = 45,000 shares. Since 47,000 is more than 45,000, the company can go ahead with allotment.\nIn a case of under-subscription, allotment is limited to the shares actually applied for.\nSo the company allots 47,000 shares. Allotting 50,000 is not possible because nobody applied for the other 3,000 shares.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-057","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":57,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"Meera holds 400 equity shares of Rs. 10 each, payable Rs. 3 on application, Rs. 3 on allotment and Rs. 4 on first and final call. She paid the application and allotment money but failed to pay the first and final call. Her shares were forfeited. With what amount will Share Forfeiture A/c be credited?","image":"","options":["Rs. 1,600","Rs. 2,400","Rs. 4,000","Rs. 1,200"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: 400 shares; amount paid per share = Rs. 3 + Rs. 3 = Rs. 6.\nRule: Share Forfeiture A/c is credited with the amount actually received on the forfeited shares.\nCalculation: 400 × Rs. 6 = Rs. 2,400.\nEntry: Share Capital Dr. Rs. 4,000 (called-up amount); To First and Final Call Rs. 1,600; To Share Forfeiture Rs. 2,400.\nRs. 1,600 is the unpaid call, not the forfeited amount.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-058","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":58,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements regarding reissue of forfeited shares is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["Forfeited shares may be reissued as fully paid at par, at a premium or at a discount.","When only a part of the forfeited shares is reissued, the entire balance of the Share Forfeiture Account is transferred to Capital Reserve.","The discount allowed on reissue cannot exceed the amount forfeited on those shares.","Share Capital Account is credited with the face value of shares reissued as fully paid."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: NCERT allows reissue as fully paid at par, at a premium or at a discount.\n(2) Incorrect: capital profit arises only on the shares actually reissued, so only the proportionate gain is transferred. The rest stays for shares not yet reissued.\n(3) Correct: the maximum discount is the amount forfeited.\n(4) Correct: on a fully paid reissue, Share Capital is credited with the full face value.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-059","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":59,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements regarding forfeiture of shares are correct?\n(i) Share Capital A/c is debited with the amount called up on the forfeited shares.\n(ii) Securities Premium Reserve A/c is debited on forfeiture even if the premium on those shares has been received.\n(iii) The balance of Share Forfeiture A/c is added to the paid-up capital under the head 'Share Capital' till the shares are reissued.\n(iv) Share Forfeiture A/c is credited with the amount received on forfeited shares, excluding premium already received.","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: Share Capital A/c is debited with the called-up amount, not the nominal value.\n(ii) Incorrect: Securities Premium Reserve A/c is debited only when the premium has not been received.\n(iii) Correct: The forfeiture balance is shown as an addition to paid-up capital under Share Capital.\n(iv) Correct: Premium already received stays in Securities Premium Reserve and is not included in the forfeited amount.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-060","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":60,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"Neel Ltd. invited applications for 50,000 equity shares of Rs. 10 each, payable as Rs. 2 on application, Rs. 3 on allotment, Rs. 3 on first call and Rs. 2 on final call. Applications were received for 1,50,000 shares. The directors:\n- rejected applications for 80,000 shares outright,\n- accepted applications for 40,000 shares in full, and\n- allotted the remaining 10,000 shares pro-rata to the applicants for 30,000 shares.\nExcess application money of the pro-rata applicants is to be adjusted towards allotment and calls, and any balance is to be refunded. What amount will be credited to Calls in Advance Account?","image":"","options":["Rs. 10,000","Rs. 40,000","Rs. 50,000","Nil"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: the pro-rata group applied for 30,000 shares and was allotted 10,000 shares.\nAmount paid by the pro-rata group = 30,000 × Rs. 2 = Rs. 60,000.\nAmount needed as application money on 10,000 shares = 10,000 × Rs. 2 = Rs. 20,000.\nExcess = Rs. 60,000 − Rs. 20,000 = Rs. 40,000.\nAllotment due on 10,000 shares = 10,000 × Rs. 3 = Rs. 30,000, leaving Rs. 10,000.\nTotal calls on 10,000 shares = Rs. 50,000, which exceeds Rs. 10,000. So the full Rs. 10,000 goes to Calls in Advance and nothing from this group is refunded.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-061","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":61,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are permitted uses of the Securities Premium Account?\n(i) Issuing fully paid bonus shares\n(ii) Writing off preliminary expenses\n(iii) Paying dividend to equity shareholders\n(iv) Paying premium on redemption of debentures\n(v) Buy-back of the company's own shares\n(vi) Purchasing fixed assets","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Permitted, provided the bonus shares do not exceed the unissued share capital.\n(ii) Permitted.\n(iii) Not permitted: securities premium is a capital receipt and cannot be distributed as dividend.\n(iv) Permitted.\n(v) Permitted.\n(vi) Not permitted: it is not one of the five specified purposes.\nSo the permitted uses are (i), (ii), (iv) and (v), which makes 4.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-062","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":62,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"Case: Vivid Ltd. forfeited 500 equity shares of Rs. 100 each held by Karan. He had paid Rs. 20 on application and Rs. 30 on allotment but failed to pay the first call of Rs. 20 per share. Out of these forfeited shares, 300 shares were reissued as fully paid at Rs. 80 per share. No other shares had been forfeited earlier.\nWhat amount should be transferred to Capital Reserve on this reissue?","image":"","options":["Rs. 9,000","Rs. 19,000","Rs. 15,000","Rs. 10,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: amount received per share = 20 + 30 = Rs. 50.\nTotal forfeited = 500 × 50 = Rs. 25,000. The forfeiture relating to the 300 reissued shares = 300 × 50 = Rs. 15,000.\nDiscount on reissue = 300 × (100 − 80) = Rs. 6,000, debited to Share Forfeiture A/c. This is within the Rs. 50 per share received.\nCapital Reserve = 15,000 − 6,000 = Rs. 9,000. Only the gain on reissued shares is transferred.\nRs. 19,000 (25,000 − 6,000) wrongly transfers the whole balance. The Rs. 10,000 relating to the 200 unissued shares stays in Share Forfeiture A/c.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-063","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":63,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"Arjun was allotted 500 equity shares of Rs. 10 each issued at Rs. 13 per share. The shares were payable Rs. 3 on application, Rs. 5 on allotment (including the premium of Rs. 3) and Rs. 5 on first and final call. He paid the application and allotment money but not the final call, and his shares were forfeited. What amount will be credited to Share Forfeiture A/c?","image":"","options":["Rs. 2,500","Rs. 4,000","Rs. 1,500","Rs. 5,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: Allotment Rs. 5 = Rs. 2 capital + Rs. 3 premium. The premium has been received.\nRule: If premium has been received, Securities Premium Reserve is not debited, and the forfeited amount excludes the premium.\nCapital received per share = Rs. 3 + Rs. 2 = Rs. 5, so 500 × Rs. 5 = Rs. 2,500.\nEntry: Share Capital Dr. Rs. 5,000; To Final Call Rs. 2,500; To Share Forfeiture Rs. 2,500.\nRs. 4,000 wrongly includes the premium.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-064","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":64,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): When shares are issued to a vendor for an asset purchased, no entry is passed through the Share Application Account or the Bank Account.\nReason (R): No cash is received for such shares; the vendor's account is debited and Share Capital Account is credited.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.","Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.","A is true, but R is false.","A is false, but R is true."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. Shares issued to a vendor are not applied for in cash, so no application or bank entry arises.\nR is true. The company owes the vendor the price of the asset. Issuing shares settles this debt, so the vendor is debited and Share Capital (and Securities Premium, if any) is credited.\nR explains why A holds.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-065","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":65,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"Asha Ltd. has a share capital of 10,000, 8% preference shares of Rs. 100 each and 50,000 equity shares of Rs. 10 each, all fully paid. Profits available for distribution as dividend for the year are Rs. 3,00,000, and the entire amount is to be distributed. What is the dividend per equity share?","image":"","options":["Rs. 6.00","Rs. 4.40","Rs. 1.60","Rs. 5.60"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: Profit = Rs. 3,00,000. Preference capital = 10,000 × Rs. 100 = Rs. 10,00,000 at 8%. There are 50,000 equity shares.\nFormula: Equity dividend = Profit − Preference dividend, because the preference dividend is paid first.\nSubstituting: Preference dividend = 8% × Rs. 10,00,000 = Rs. 80,000. Balance = Rs. 3,00,000 − Rs. 80,000 = Rs. 2,20,000.\nPer equity share = Rs. 2,20,000 ÷ 50,000 = Rs. 4.40.\nOption (1) ignores the preference dividend, which is a common error.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-066","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":66,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"At the time of forfeiting shares for non-payment of calls, the Share Capital Account is debited with:","image":"","options":["The amount actually paid up on the forfeited shares","The amount called up on the forfeited shares","The full face value of the forfeited shares, whether called up or not","The amount received on the forfeited shares, including premium"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Forfeiture reverses the entries made for the forfeited shares.\nShare Capital was credited each time an instalment became due, so it is debited with the called-up amount.\nThe paid-up amount is credited to Share Forfeiture A/c, and the unpaid part is credited to the call accounts or Calls in Arrears A/c.\nOption (1) is wrong because the paid-up amount is the forfeiture credit, not the capital debit.\nOption (3) is wrong because uncalled capital was never credited to Share Capital.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-067","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":67,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II:\nList-I\n(A) Company limited by shares\n(B) Company limited by guarantee\n(C) Unlimited company\n(D) Public company\nList-II\n(i) Not a private company and not a subsidiary of a private company\n(ii) Private property of members can be used to pay company's debts\n(iii) Liability limited to the amount members undertake to contribute on winding up\n(iv) Liability limited to the unpaid amount on shares held","image":"","options":["A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(ii), D-(i)","A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(ii), D-(i)","A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(ii), B-(iii), C-(iv), D-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A)-(iv): In a company limited by shares, a member is liable only for the amount unpaid on his shares.\n(B)-(iii): In a company limited by guarantee, a member is liable only up to the amount he undertook to contribute on winding up.\n(C)-(ii): In an unlimited company, creditors can claim from members' private property.\n(D)-(i): A public company is one that is not a private company and not a subsidiary of a private company.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-068","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":68,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"Arun Ltd. issued equity shares of Rs. 10 each at a premium of Rs. 2 per share. The amount was payable as Rs. 3 on application, Rs. 5 on allotment (including premium) and Rs. 4 on first and final call. 300 shares were forfeited for non-payment of the first and final call. Out of these, 200 shares were reissued at Rs. 8 per share as fully paid. What amount will be transferred to Capital Reserve?","image":"","options":["Rs. 800","Rs. 1,200","Rs. 1,400","Rs. 1,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: the premium was received on the forfeited shares, so it is excluded from the forfeited amount.\nForfeited amount per share = 3 + (5 − 2) = Rs. 6. Total forfeiture = 300 × 6 = Rs. 1,800.\nForfeiture relating to the 200 reissued shares = 200 × 6 = Rs. 1,200.\nDiscount on reissue = 200 × (10 − 8) = Rs. 400, debited to Share Forfeiture A/c.\nCapital Reserve = 1,200 − 400 = Rs. 800. Only the reissued shares' portion is transferred, which is why Rs. 1,400 (1,800 − 400) is wrong.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-069","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":69,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): When shares issued at a premium are forfeited for non-payment of the final call, Securities Premium Reserve Account is not debited if the premium was received with allotment.\nReason (R): On forfeiture, all entries relating to the forfeited shares are reversed except the entry for share premium already received.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: the premium was collected in full, so the Securities Premium Reserve stays intact. The forfeited amount also excludes the premium.\nR is true: it states the NCERT rule that every entry for forfeited shares is reversed except the premium already received.\nR is exactly the reason Securities Premium is not debited in A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-070","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":70,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"Neel Ltd. allotted 50,000 equity shares of Rs. 10 each and has called up Rs. 8 per share. Shareholders holding 2,000 shares have not paid the last call of Rs. 2 per share. What is the paid-up capital of Neel Ltd.?","image":"","options":["Rs. 4,00,000","Rs. 3,96,000","Rs. 5,00,000","Rs. 4,04,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: 50,000 shares with Rs. 8 called up. Calls in arrears = 2,000 × Rs. 2 = Rs. 4,000.\nFormula: Paid-up capital = Called-up capital − Calls in arrears.\nSubstituting: called-up capital = 50,000 × 8 = Rs. 4,00,000; paid-up capital = 4,00,000 − 4,000.\nSo paid-up capital = Rs. 3,96,000.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-071","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":71,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"Ravi holds 2,000 equity shares of Mira Ltd. He failed to pay the first call of Rs. 3 per share, which was due on 1st June 2024. He paid the call money along with interest on 30th September 2024. The Articles of Association are silent on interest, so Table F applies. Interest is charged at the maximum rate allowed. What amount of interest will Mira Ltd. receive from Ravi?","image":"","options":["Rs. 200","Rs. 240","Rs. 600","Rs. 150"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: Calls in arrears = 2,000 × Rs. 3 = Rs. 6,000. Period = 1 June to 30 Sept = 4 months.\nFormula: Table F allows interest on calls in arrears at a rate not exceeding 10% p.a. So Interest = Amount × 10% × 4/12.\nSubstituting: 6,000 × 10/100 × 4/12 = Rs. 200.\nRs. 240 is wrong because it uses 12% p.a., which is the Table F limit for calls in advance, not calls in arrears.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-072","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":72,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): A company offered 1,00,000 shares to the public but received applications for only 85,000 shares. It cannot proceed to allot these 85,000 shares.\nReason (R): According to SEBI guidelines, minimum subscription cannot be less than 90% of the issued amount.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"R is true: SEBI guidelines require minimum subscription of at least 90% of the issued amount.\nA is true: 85,000 shares is only 85% of the shares offered. This is below the minimum subscription, so the company cannot allot and must refund the entire subscription received.\nThe allotment fails precisely because the 90% requirement is not met. So R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-073","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":73,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): A company may issue equity share capital with differential rights as to voting or dividend.\nReason (R): Under the Companies Act, 2013, every equity share must carry exactly identical voting rights.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true. Equity share capital may be (i) with voting rights, or (ii) with differential rights as to voting, dividend or otherwise.\nSuch differential rights must follow the prescribed rules and the conditions laid down in the Articles of Association.\nR is false. The very fact that differential voting rights are allowed shows that equity shares need not carry identical voting rights.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-074","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":74,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"Riya held 600 equity shares of Rs. 10 each issued at a premium of Rs. 2 per share. She paid only the application money of Rs. 3 per share. She failed to pay the allotment money of Rs. 5 (including premium Rs. 2) and the final call of Rs. 4, and her shares were forfeited. Later, 400 of these shares were reissued at Rs. 8 per share as fully paid. What amount will be transferred to Capital Reserve?","image":"","options":["Rs. 400","Rs. 1,200","Rs. 1,000","Rs. 800"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: the premium was not received, so it is reversed. Forfeiture = amount received = 600 × 3 = Rs. 1,800.\nForfeiture relating to the 400 reissued shares = 400 × 3 = Rs. 1,200.\nDiscount on reissue = 400 × (10 − 8) = Rs. 800, debited to Share Forfeiture A/c.\nCapital Reserve = 1,200 − 800 = Rs. 400. A balance of Rs. 600 remains for the 200 shares not reissued.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-075","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":75,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about One Person Company (OPC) under the Companies (Incorporation) Rules, 2014 is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["Only a natural person who is an Indian citizen and resident in India can form an OPC","Its paid-up share capital is not more than Rs. 50 lakhs","It can carry out non-banking financial investment activities","Its average annual turnover of three years does not exceed Rs. 2 crores"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: only an Indian citizen who is a natural person and resident in India can form an OPC.\n(2) Correct: paid-up capital must not exceed Rs. 50 lakhs.\n(3) Incorrect: Rule 3 specifically says an OPC CANNOT carry out non-banking financial investment activities.\n(4) Correct: average annual turnover of three years must not exceed Rs. 2 crores.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-076","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":76,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"Under the Companies Act, 2013, Securities Premium Reserve can be used for certain specified purposes only. Which of the following is NOT one of them?","image":"","options":["Issuing fully paid bonus shares to shareholders","Writing off preliminary expenses of the company","Paying dividend to equity shareholders","Buy-back of its own shares"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT lists five permitted uses of securities premium:\n(a) issuing fully paid bonus shares\n(b) writing off preliminary expenses\n(c) writing off expenses of, commission on, or discount on the issue of securities\n(d) paying premium on redemption of preference shares or debentures\n(e) buy-back of own shares.\nPaying dividend is not on this list. Premium is a capital receipt, not a distributable profit.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-077","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":77,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"A company rejects some applications outright because of over-subscription. Which journal entry records the refund of application money on these rejected applications?","image":"","options":["Share Capital A/c Dr. To Bank A/c","Bank A/c Dr. To Share Application A/c","Share Application A/c Dr. To Share Allotment A/c","Share Application A/c Dr. To Bank A/c"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"A refund reduces the money held in the Share Application Account, which is a liability to applicants. So that account is debited.\nCash goes out of the company, so Bank is credited.\nOption (2) is the entry for receiving application money, not refunding it.\nOption (3) is used to adjust excess money towards allotment under pro-rata allotment, not for a refund.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-078","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":78,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) A private company must have at least 2 persons, except in the case of a One Person Company.\n(ii) The paid-up share capital of a One Person Company is not more than Rs. 50 lakh.\n(iii) The average annual turnover of a One Person Company over three years does not exceed Rs. 2 crore.\n(iv) A One Person Company may carry out non-banking financial investment activities.\n(v) The consent of a member of a public limited company is necessary for the transfer of its shares.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: A private company needs at least 2 persons, except an OPC.\n(ii) Correct: An OPC's paid-up share capital is not more than Rs. 50 lakh.\n(iii) Correct: An OPC's average annual turnover over three years does not exceed Rs. 2 crore.\n(iv) Incorrect: An OPC cannot carry out non-banking financial investment activities.\n(v) Incorrect: Shares of a public limited company are freely transferable without the consent of the company or any member.\nSo three statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-079","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":79,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"Neelgiri Textiles Ltd. issued equity shares of Rs. 10 each at par, payable Rs. 2 on application, Rs. 3 on allotment, and the balance in three equal calls. Which account is debited when the money due on the last instalment is recorded?","image":"","options":["Equity Share Final Call A/c","Equity Share Third and Final Call A/c","Equity Share Third Call A/c","Equity Share Capital A/c"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Each call account is named by its serial number: First, Second, Third, and so on.\nThe words 'and Final' are added to the name of the last call.\nHere the third call is the last instalment, so the account is 'Equity Share Third and Final Call A/c'.\nThe prefix 'Equity' is added because the issue is of equity shares.\nOption (3) leaves out 'and Final'. Option (4) is the account credited in this entry, not debited.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-080","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":80,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"Zenith Ltd. has no Articles of Association of its own, so Table F applies. It issued shares of Rs. 100 each, payable Rs. 20 on application, Rs. 30 on allotment and the balance through calls. Which of the following call plans complies with Table F?","image":"","options":["One first and final call of Rs. 50, with 21 days' notice","Two calls of Rs. 25 each, made two weeks apart, each with 14 days' notice","Two calls of Rs. 25 each, made two months apart, each with 21 days' notice","Two calls of Rs. 25 each, made two months apart, each with 7 days' notice"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Table F rules: no call above 25% of face value (Rs. 25 here); at least one month between two calls; at least 14 days' notice; calls uniform within a class.\n(1) Fails: Rs. 50 is 50% of face value.\n(2) Fails: a two-week gap is less than one month.\n(3) Complies: Rs. 25 is within the limit, the gap is two months, and 21 days' notice is at least 14 days.\n(4) Fails: 7 days' notice is less than 14 days.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-081","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":81,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): When shares issued at a premium are forfeited for non-payment of allotment money (which included the premium), Securities Premium Reserve Account is debited in the forfeiture entry.\nReason (R): Securities Premium Reserve can be used to write off the preliminary expenses of the company.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A is true. The premium was credited when allotment fell due, but it was never received on these shares. On forfeiture that credit is reversed by debiting Securities Premium Reserve.\nR is also true, since writing off preliminary expenses is a permitted use of securities premium.\nHowever, R does not explain A. The debit is made because the premium was not received, not because of how premium may be used.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-082","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":82,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"Read the following statements:\nStatement I: A company can reissue forfeited shares at a discount, even though it cannot ordinarily issue shares at a discount.\nStatement II: The discount allowed on reissue of forfeited shares may exceed the amount that was forfeited on those shares.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I – Correct: NCERT names reissue of forfeited shares as one of the cases in which shares can be issued at a discount.\nStatement II – Incorrect: the discount on reissue cannot exceed the amount received on those shares at the time of the original issue, that is, the amount forfeited.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-083","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":83,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"Under Rule 3 of the Companies (Incorporation) Rules, 2014, who can form a One Person Company (OPC)?","image":"","options":["Any company registered in India","A natural person who is an Indian citizen and resident in India","Any natural person, whether or not an Indian citizen","A partnership firm registered in India"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Section 2(62) defines an OPC as a company with only one person as a member.\nRule 3 adds that only a natural person who is an Indian citizen and resident in India can form an OPC.\nA company or a firm is not a natural person, so options (1) and (4) fail.\nA foreign citizen does not meet the citizenship condition, so option (3) fails.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-084","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":84,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"Sona Ltd. purchased a machine from Delta Ltd. for Rs. 4,80,000. It paid Rs. 30,000 in cash. The balance was settled by issuing equity shares of Rs. 100 each at a premium of 25%. With what amount will Securities Premium Reserve Account be credited?","image":"","options":["Rs. 1,12,500","Rs. 1,20,000","Rs. 90,000","Rs. 72,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: amount payable in shares = 4,80,000 − 30,000 = Rs. 4,50,000.\nIssue price = 100 + 25% of 100 = Rs. 125.\nNumber of shares = Amount payable ÷ Issue price = 4,50,000 ÷ 125 = 3,600 shares.\nPremium = 3,600 × 25 = Rs. 90,000.\nEntry: Delta Ltd. Dr. 4,50,000; To Share Capital A/c 3,60,000; To Securities Premium Reserve A/c 90,000.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-085","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":85,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about share capital are correct?\n(i) Authorised capital is also known as nominal or registered capital.\n(ii) Issued capital includes shares allotted to vendors and to the signatories of the memorandum.\n(iii) Paid-up capital can be more than called-up capital.\n(iv) Unissued capital may be offered for public subscription at a later date.\n(v) A company must issue its entire authorised capital at one time.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: authorised capital is also called nominal or registered capital.\n(ii) Correct: issued capital includes shares allotted to vendors and to memorandum signatories.\n(iii) Incorrect: paid-up capital equals called-up capital minus calls in arrears, so it can never exceed called-up capital.\n(iv) Correct: unissued capital can be issued later.\n(v) Incorrect: a company may issue capital as it needs it, within the authorised limit.\nSo 3 statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-086","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":86,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"Read the following statements about Securities Premium:\nStatement I: The balance of Securities Premium can be used to write off the preliminary expenses of the company.\nStatement II: The balance of Securities Premium can be used to pay dividend to equity shareholders.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Writing off preliminary expenses is one of the five permitted uses of securities premium.\nThe five permitted uses are: issuing fully paid bonus shares, writing off preliminary expenses, writing off issue expenses, commission or discount on securities, paying premium on redemption of preference shares or debentures, and buy-back of shares.\nStatement II is incorrect. Payment of dividend is not on this list. Securities premium is a capital receipt, not distributable profit.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-087","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":87,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"Delta Ltd. invited applications for 60,000 shares and received applications for 90,000 shares. The directors made a pro-rata allotment to all applicants. Meena was allotted 1,200 shares. How many shares had she applied for?","image":"","options":["1,200 shares","1,800 shares","2,400 shares","800 shares"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: shares allotted = 60,000 and shares applied for = 90,000.\nPro-rata ratio (allotted : applied) = 60,000 : 90,000 = 2 : 3.\nShares applied for = shares allotted × 3/2 = 1,200 × 3/2.\nSo Meena applied for 1,800 shares.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-088","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":88,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (situation in an over-subscribed issue) with List-II (accounting treatment of the application money concerned):\nList-I\n(A) Application money on applications rejected outright\n(B) Application money on the number of shares actually allotted\n(C) Excess application money of pro-rata allottees, up to the amount due on allotment\n(D) Excess application money of pro-rata allottees left after meeting allotment, when the directors decide to retain it\nList-II\n(i) Credited to Share Capital A/c\n(ii) Credited to Calls in Advance A/c\n(iii) Credited to Bank A/c (refunded)\n(iv) Credited to Share Allotment A/c","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(i), C-(iv), D-(ii)","A-(i), B-(iii), C-(iv), D-(ii)","A-(iii), B-(i), C-(ii), D-(iv)","A-(iv), B-(i), C-(iii), D-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Rejected applications: the money is returned in full, so Bank A/c is credited. A matches (iii).\n(B) Money on shares allotted becomes part of share capital, so Share Capital A/c is credited. B matches (i).\n(C) Excess money of pro-rata allottees is first adjusted against allotment, so Share Allotment A/c is credited. C matches (iv).\n(D) Any excess left after allotment may be refunded or retained. If retained, Calls in Advance A/c is credited. D matches (ii).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-089","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":89,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"Read the following statements:\nStatement I: The balance of Calls in Advance Account is shown under 'Other current liabilities' and is not added to the paid-up capital.\nStatement II: If the Articles are silent, Table F allows interest on calls in advance at a rate not exceeding 12% p.a.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Calls in advance are a liability of the company. They appear under Current Liabilities, sub-head 'Other current liabilities'. They are not added to paid-up capital.\nStatement II is correct. When the Articles are silent, Table F permits interest on calls in advance at a rate not exceeding 12% p.a.\nDo not confuse this with calls in arrears, which carry a maximum of 10%.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-090","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":90,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The part of authorised capital that has not been offered for public subscription is called unissued capital, and it may be offered later.\nStatement II: The part of issued capital that has not been subscribed by the public is called uncalled capital.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct: authorised capital not offered for subscription is unissued capital, and it can be offered at a later date.\nStatement II is incorrect: uncalled capital is the part of subscribed capital that has not yet been called up. It has nothing to do with shares the public did not subscribe.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-091","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":91,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"In which document does a company state the maximum amount of share capital it is allowed to raise, also called its nominal or registered capital?","image":"","options":["Articles of Association","Prospectus","Memorandum of Association","Table F of the Companies Act"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Authorised capital is the maximum share capital a company may issue, and it is stated in the Memorandum of Association.\nIt is also called nominal or registered capital.\nThe Articles of Association set out the classes of shares and their rights, not the ceiling on capital.\nThe prospectus only invites the public to subscribe.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-092","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":92,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about the issue of shares at par are correct?\n(i) The accounting procedure for issuing equity shares and preference shares is the same; only the words 'Equity' or 'Preference' are prefixed to each instalment account.\n(ii) Application money should be at least 5% of the face value of the share.\n(iii) Under Table A, a call may exceed 25% of the face value if the company gives 14 days' notice.\n(iv) In the Notes to Accounts, calls in arrears are deducted from the called-up amount of shares that are subscribed but not fully paid up.","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: NCERT states the procedure is the same, with only the prefixes differing.\n(ii) Correct: application money must be at least 5% of the face value.\n(iii) Incorrect: under Table A, a call cannot exceed 25% of the face value; the 14 days' notice is a separate requirement.\n(iv) Correct: this is how the Unique Pictures notes show it.\nSo 3 statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-093","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":93,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"A company collects Rs. 3 on application and Rs. 3 on allotment on its shares of Rs. 10 each. It decides to collect the remaining Rs. 4 in a single call. What should this call account be named?","image":"","options":["Share Final Call Account","Share Second and Final Call Account","Share First and Final Call Account","Share Balance Call Account"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The word First, Second and so on is placed between 'Share' and 'Call', and 'and Final' is added to the last call.\nWhen the whole balance after allotment is collected in one call, that call is both the first and the last call.\nSo it is named 'Share First and Final Call Account', as with Eastern Company Ltd.\nOption (2) would apply only if an earlier first call had already been made.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-094","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":94,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (transaction) with List-II (journal entry):\nList-I\n(A) Calls in advance adjusted when the call becomes due\n(B) Calls in arrears received along with interest\n(C) Interest paid on calls in advance\n(D) Unpaid first call brought into a separate account\nList-II\n(i) Interest on Calls in Advance A/c Dr. To Bank A/c\n(ii) Calls in Arrears A/c Dr. To Share First Call A/c\n(iii) Calls in Advance A/c Dr. To Share Call A/c\n(iv) Bank A/c Dr. To Calls in Arrears A/c, To Interest on Calls in Arrears A/c","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(ii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(iii)","A-(iii), B-(i), C-(iv), D-(ii)","A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(ii), D-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A)-(iii): The liability is reduced by debiting Calls in Advance and crediting the call account.\n(B)-(iv): Bank is debited; Calls in Arrears and the interest account are credited.\n(C)-(i): Interest on calls in advance is an expense, paid from the bank.\n(D)-(ii): The unpaid call balance is moved to the Calls in Arrears Account.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-095","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":95,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"A contract document of a company was signed by its manager but did not bear the company's common seal. According to NCERT, what is the status of this document?","image":"","options":["It is binding on the company because the manager signed it","It is binding only on the shareholders","It is not binding on the company","It is binding only if the company is a private company"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A company is an artificial person, so it cannot sign its own name.\nIts common seal acts as its official signature.\nNCERT states that any document which does not carry the common seal is not binding on the company.\nA manager's signature alone does not replace the seal. The type of company makes no difference to this rule.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-096","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":96,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"Mehta Ltd. issued shares of Rs. 10 each at Rs. 12, payable Rs. 3 on application, Rs. 5 on allotment (including premium of Rs. 2) and Rs. 4 on first and final call. A shareholder holding 300 shares paid the application and allotment money but failed to pay the call, and his shares were forfeited. Later, 200 of these shares were reissued as fully paid at Rs. 9 per share. What amount will be transferred to Capital Reserve?","image":"","options":["Rs. 1,600","Rs. 1,000","Rs. 1,200","Rs. 1,400"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: The amount paid excluding premium is 3 + 3 = Rs. 6 per share. The premium was received, so it is not forfeited.\nShare forfeiture = 300 × 6 = Rs. 1,800. The share relating to the 200 reissued shares = 200 × 6 = Rs. 1,200.\nDiscount on reissue = (10 − 9) × 200 = Rs. 200, which is debited to Share Forfeiture.\nCapital Reserve = 1,200 − 200 = Rs. 1,000.\nRs. 1,400 wrongly includes the premium in the forfeited amount.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-097","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":97,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"On 31st March, Zenith Ltd. holds Rs. 15,000 that shareholders paid towards a second call. The company has not yet made that call. Where will this amount appear in the company's Balance Sheet?","image":"","options":["Shareholders' Funds – Share Capital, added to paid-up capital","Shareholders' Funds – Reserves and Surplus","Current Liabilities – Other Current Liabilities","Current Assets – Other Current Assets"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Money received before a call is made is called 'Calls in Advance'.\nNCERT treats Calls in Advance as a liability of the company. It is shown under Current Liabilities, sub-head 'Other Current Liabilities'.\nIt is NOT added to paid-up capital, because the call has not yet become due. This rules out option (1), the most tempting distractor.\nIt is not a reserve, so (2) is wrong. It is not an asset, so (4) is wrong.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-098","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":98,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (Kind of company) with List-II (Characteristic):\nList-I\nA. Company limited by guarantee\nB. Unlimited company\nC. Private company\nD. One Person Company\nList-II\n(i) Restricts the right to transfer its shares and limits members to 200 (excluding employees)\n(ii) Paid-up share capital not more than Rs. 50 lakh\n(iii) Members' liability arises only on winding up, up to the amount they undertook to contribute\n(iv) Creditors can recover their dues from the private property of members\nChoose the correct option:","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(ii), D-(i)","A-(i), B-(iv), C-(iii), D-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A-(iii): In a company limited by guarantee, members are liable only for the amount they undertake to contribute. This liability arises only when the company is wound up.\nB-(iv): An unlimited company puts no limit on members' liability. If the company's property is not enough to pay its debts, creditors can recover from the members' private property.\nC-(i): A private company restricts the transfer of its shares. It needs at least 2 members and can have at most 200, excluding employees.\nD-(ii): Under Rule 3, a One Person Company's paid-up capital cannot exceed Rs. 50 lakh.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-099","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":99,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"Read the two statements about the journal entry for forfeiture of shares.\nStatement I: The unpaid allotment and call accounts (or the Calls in Arrears A/c, if maintained) are credited with the amounts not received on the forfeited shares.\nStatement II: Share Forfeiture A/c carries a debit balance until the forfeited shares are reissued.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. On forfeiture, the entries relating to the forfeited shares are reversed. The unpaid allotment and call accounts, or Calls in Arrears A/c, are credited with the amounts still unpaid.\nStatement II is incorrect. Share Forfeiture A/c is credited with the amount already received, so it carries a credit balance. This balance is added to paid-up capital until the shares are reissued.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-100","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":100,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements regarding over-subscription and ASBA is correct?","image":"","options":["Under ASBA, the bank debits the applicant's account with the full application money on the day the application is submitted.","In a pro-rata allotment, any excess application money left after adjusting the allotment amount must compulsorily be refunded.","When an issue is over-subscribed, the subscribed capital in the books equals the number of shares applied for multiplied by the face value.","Under ASBA, if securities are not allotted, the bank removes the lien and no amount is debited to the applicant's account."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(1) Incorrect. Under ASBA the amount is only blocked (a lien is marked). The account is debited only for securities actually allotted.\n(2) Incorrect. Such surplus may either be refunded or credited to Calls in Advance.\n(3) Incorrect. Allotment cannot exceed the offer, so over-subscription is not reflected in the books.\n(4) Correct. If there is no allotment, the bank simply removes the block on the amount.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-101","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":101,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"Sagar Ltd. invited applications for 20,000 equity shares of Rs. 10 each, with Rs. 3 payable on application. Applications were received for 26,000 shares. The directors accepted applications for 20,000 shares in full and rejected the rest outright. What amount of application money will be refunded?","image":"","options":["Rs. 18,000","Rs. 60,000","Rs. 78,000","Rs. 6,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: shares applied for = 26,000; shares allotted = 20,000; application money = Rs. 3 per share.\nUnder full acceptance with outright rejection, the money on rejected applications is refunded in full.\nRejected applications = 26,000 − 20,000 = 6,000 shares.\nRefund = 6,000 × Rs. 3 = Rs. 18,000. The entry is: Share Application A/c Dr. To Share Capital A/c (Rs. 60,000), To Bank A/c (Rs. 18,000).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-102","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":102,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"Under an ESOP, Neha, an employee of Arjun Ltd., is given the option to buy shares at an exercise price of Rs. 110 per share. On that date the market price of the share is Rs. 150. What is the value of the option per share?","image":"","options":["Rs. 150","Rs. 110","Rs. 40","Rs. 260"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: market price = Rs. 150; exercise (issue) price = Rs. 110.\nFormula: Value of option = Market price − Issue price.\nSubstituting: 150 − 110 = 40.\nSo the value of the option = Rs. 40 per share.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-103","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":103,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"Tara Ltd. issued 30,000 equity shares of Rs. 10 each at a premium of Rs. 2. The amounts were payable as follows: Rs. 3 on application, Rs. 5 on allotment (including premium) and Rs. 4 on first and final call. Applications were received for 36,000 shares, and a pro-rata allotment was made to all applicants. Excess application money was adjusted towards allotment. Meena, who was allotted 500 shares, paid nothing beyond her application money, and her shares were forfeited after the call. What is the total of the debit side of the forfeiture journal entry?","image":"","options":["Rs. 5,000","Rs. 6,000","Rs. 5,800","Rs. 6,300"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: ratio 30,000 : 36,000 = 5 : 6, so Meena applied for 600 shares and paid 600 x 3 = Rs. 1,800.\nDebits: Share Capital 500 x 10 = Rs. 5,000. Securities Premium 500 x 2 = Rs. 1,000, because the premium was unpaid.\nCredits: Allotment (500 x 5 - 100 x 3) = Rs. 2,200; Call 500 x 4 = Rs. 2,000; Share Forfeiture Rs. 1,800.\nTotal debit = 5,000 + 1,000 = Rs. 6,000, which equals the total credit of Rs. 6,000.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-104","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":104,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (situation at the time of forfeiture) with List-II (accounting treatment):\nList-I\nA. Premium was received but the final call is unpaid\nB. Allotment money including premium is unpaid\nC. Company maintains a Calls in Arrears Account\nD. Amount already received on the forfeited shares\nList-II\n(i) Securities Premium Reserve A/c is debited with the unpaid premium\n(ii) Securities Premium Reserve A/c is not debited\n(iii) Calls in Arrears A/c is credited instead of the individual call accounts\n(iv) Credited to Share Forfeiture A/c","image":"","options":["A-(ii), B-(i), C-(iii), D-(iv)","A-(i), B-(ii), C-(iii), D-(iv)","A-(ii), B-(i), C-(iv), D-(iii)","A-(iv), B-(i), C-(iii), D-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A-(ii): Premium already received is not reversed.\nB-(i): Unpaid premium was credited to Securities Premium earlier, so it is now debited back.\nC-(iii): NCERT notes that when a Calls in Arrears A/c is kept, it is credited instead of the allotment or call accounts.\nD-(iv): Money received on the forfeited shares is credited to Share Forfeiture A/c.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-105","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":105,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"Case: Orion Ltd. issued 10,000 equity shares of Rs. 10 each on these terms:\n- Rs. 3 on application\n- Rs. 3 on allotment\n- Rs. 2 on first call\n- Rs. 2 on second and final call\n\nAll shares were allotted. Mr. P holds 500 shares. Along with his allotment money, he paid the entire balance of both calls. The company credited the excess to Calls in Advance A/c.\n\nWhen the first call was made:\n- The amount due from Mr. P was adjusted from Calls in Advance.\n- Ms. Q, holding 300 shares, failed to pay the first call.\n- All other shareholders paid in full.\n\nWhat amount will be debited to Bank A/c in the entry for receipt of the first call money?","image":"","options":["Rs. 18,400","Rs. 19,400","Rs. 17,400","Rs. 19,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: First call due = 10,000 × Rs. 2 = Rs. 20,000.\nCalls in advance adjusted for Mr. P = 500 × Rs. 2 = Rs. 1,000. Of his advance of Rs. 2,000, the other Rs. 1,000 stays for the final call.\nCalls in arrears for Ms. Q = 300 × Rs. 2 = Rs. 600.\nFormula: Bank = Due − Advance adjusted − Arrears.\nSubstituting: 20,000 − 1,000 − 600 = Rs. 18,400.\nThe entry is: Bank Dr. 18,400, Calls in Advance Dr. 1,000 and Calls in Arrears Dr. 600; To First Call 20,000.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-106","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":106,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"Ritu Ltd. acquired assets worth Rs. 9,00,000 from Om Traders. It paid the full price by issuing equity shares of Rs. 10 each at Rs. 12.50 per share. What amount will be credited to Securities Premium Reserve Account?","image":"","options":["Rs. 2,25,000","Rs. 1,44,000","Rs. 7,20,000","Rs. 1,80,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Given: Amount payable = Rs. 9,00,000; issue price = Rs. 12.50; face value = Rs. 10.\nNumber of shares = 9,00,000 ÷ 12.50 = 72,000.\nPremium per share = 12.50 − 10 = Rs. 2.50.\nSecurities Premium = 72,000 × 2.50 = Rs. 1,80,000. Share Capital gets Rs. 7,20,000.\nRs. 2,25,000 wrongly takes 25% of the price as premium.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-107","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":107,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"Neel Ltd. had forfeited 100 equity shares of Rs. 10 each on which Rs. 6 per share had been received. These shares are now reissued at Rs. 12 per share as fully paid. Which journal entry correctly records the reissue (before any transfer to Capital Reserve)?","image":"","options":["Bank A/c Dr. 1,200; To Share Capital A/c 1,200","Bank A/c Dr. 1,200; To Share Capital A/c 1,000; To Securities Premium Reserve A/c 200","Bank A/c Dr. 1,200; To Share Capital A/c 1,000; To Share Forfeiture A/c 200","Bank A/c Dr. 1,200; Share Forfeiture A/c Dr. 600; To Share Capital A/c 1,800"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Reissue as fully paid means Share Capital is credited with the face value: 100 × 10 = Rs. 1,000.\nThe excess of Rs. 2 × 100 = Rs. 200 is a premium, credited to Securities Premium Reserve, not to Share Forfeiture.\nNo discount is allowed, so Share Forfeiture A/c is not debited in this entry.\nThe forfeiture balance of Rs. 600 is then transferred to Capital Reserve in a separate entry.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-108","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":108,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II:\nList-I\n(A) Authorised capital\n(B) Issued capital\n(C) Called-up capital\n(D) Calls in arrears\nList-II\n(i) Amount the company has asked shareholders to pay on subscribed shares\n(ii) Amount called up but not paid by shareholders\n(iii) Maximum capital permitted by the Memorandum of Association\n(iv) Part of authorised capital offered for subscription, including shares allotted to vendors and signatories","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(ii), D-(i)","A-(i), B-(iv), C-(iii), D-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Authorised capital is the ceiling stated in the Memorandum, so A-(iii).\n(B) Issued capital is the part offered for subscription, including shares allotted to vendors and signatories, so B-(iv).\n(C) Called-up capital is what the company has asked shareholders to pay, so C-(i).\n(D) Calls in arrears are amounts called but unpaid, so D-(ii).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-109","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":109,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"Kavya holds 400 shares of Rs. 10 each in Nimbus Ltd., a company limited by shares. Rs. 6 per share has been called up, and she has paid it in full. The company is now being wound up, and its assets are not enough to pay its creditors. What is the maximum further amount Kavya can be asked to pay?","image":"","options":["Rs. 2,400","Rs. 4,000","Rs. 1,600","Nil"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: 400 shares, face value Rs. 10 each, Rs. 6 per share already paid.\nRule: A member's liability is limited to the unpaid amount on the shares held. Her private property cannot be touched.\nSubstituting: Unpaid amount per share = 10 − 6 = Rs. 4. Total = 400 × 4 = Rs. 1,600.\nRs. 2,400 is the amount she has already paid. Rs. 4,000 is the full nominal value.\nSo her maximum further liability = Rs. 1,600.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-110","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":110,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"Zeta Ltd. invited applications for 50,000 equity shares of Rs. 10 each, payable as Rs. 5 on application, Rs. 2 on allotment, Rs. 2 on first call and Rs. 1 on final call. Applications were received for 1,20,000 shares. The directors:\n- rejected applications for 50,000 shares outright,\n- accepted applications for 40,000 shares in full, and\n- allotted the remaining 10,000 shares pro-rata to applicants for 30,000 shares.\nThe excess application money was first used for allotment, then credited to Calls in Advance for both calls, and any balance was refunded. What is the total amount refunded?","image":"","options":["Rs. 2,50,000","Rs. 3,00,000","Rs. 3,30,000","Rs. 3,50,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Refund on rejected applications = 50,000 × Rs. 5 = Rs. 2,50,000.\nExcess from pro-rata applicants = (30,000 − 10,000) × Rs. 5 = Rs. 1,00,000.\nAdjusted to allotment = 10,000 × Rs. 2 = Rs. 20,000.\nCredited to Calls in Advance = 10,000 × (2 + 1) = Rs. 30,000.\nBalance refunded = 1,00,000 − 20,000 − 30,000 = Rs. 50,000.\nTotal refund = 2,50,000 + 50,000 = Rs. 3,00,000.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-111","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":111,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (terms used in an Employees Stock Option Plan) with List-II (meanings).\nList-I:\n(A) Grant\n(B) Vesting Period\n(C) Exercise Price\n(D) Value of Option\nList-II:\n(i) Difference between the market price and the issue price of the security\n(ii) Giving employees an option to subscribe to the company's shares at a pre-determined price\n(iii) Period between the grant date and the date by which all specified vesting conditions must be satisfied\n(iv) Price payable by the employee for exercising the option granted\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["A-(ii), B-(iii), C-(iv), D-(i)","A-(ii), B-(iv), C-(iii), D-(i)","A-(iii), B-(ii), C-(i), D-(iv)","A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(ii), D-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Grant: the company gives employees the option to subscribe at a pre-determined price, so A matches (ii).\n(B) Vesting period: the time from the grant date until all vesting conditions are satisfied, so B matches (iii).\n(C) Exercise price: the price the employee pays when exercising the option, so C matches (iv).\n(D) Value of option: market price minus issue price, so D matches (i).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-112","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":112,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"Kappa Ltd. issued 30,000 equity shares of Rs. 10 each at a premium of Rs. 2, payable as Rs. 3 on application, Rs. 5 on allotment (including premium) and Rs. 4 on call. Applications were received for 45,000 shares. All applicants were allotted shares pro-rata, and the excess application money was adjusted towards allotment. Ravi, who was allotted 600 shares, did not pay the allotment money. Everyone else paid. What amount did the company receive on allotment?","image":"","options":["Rs. 1,05,000","Rs. 1,47,900","Rs. 1,02,000","Rs. 1,02,900"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Allotment due = 30,000 × Rs. 5 = Rs. 1,50,000.\nExcess application money = 15,000 × Rs. 3 = Rs. 45,000. Net allotment due = Rs. 1,05,000.\nThe pro-rata ratio is 2 : 3, so Ravi applied for 900 shares. His excess = 300 × Rs. 3 = Rs. 900.\nRavi's unpaid amount = 600 × 5 − 900 = Rs. 2,100.\nAmount received = 1,05,000 − 2,100 = Rs. 1,02,900.\nOption (3) wrongly deducts Rs. 3,000, ignoring Ravi's own excess money.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-113","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":113,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"Arjun Ltd. has no Articles of Association of its own, so Table F provisions apply. It issues shares of Rs. 50 each, payable Rs. 10 on application and Rs. 15 on allotment. The balance is to be collected through calls. What is the minimum number of calls the company must make?","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: face value = Rs. 50; application + allotment = Rs. 10 + Rs. 15 = Rs. 25.\nBalance to be called = Rs. 50 − Rs. 25 = Rs. 25.\nRule: a single call cannot exceed 25% of face value, i.e., 25% × 50 = Rs. 12.50.\nNumber of calls needed = Rs. 25 ÷ Rs. 12.50 = 2.\nA single call of Rs. 25 (option 1) would be 50% of face value, which is not allowed.\nSo the minimum number of calls = 2.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-114","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":114,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"Shareholders sometimes pay call money before the company has made the call. Where does the balance of the Calls in Advance Account appear in the company's balance sheet?","image":"","options":["As an addition to paid-up capital under 'Share Capital'","Under 'Reserves and Surplus'","Under 'Current Liabilities', sub-head 'Other Current Liabilities'","Under 'Current Assets' as 'Other Current Assets'"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Calls in advance is money received before a call is due, so the company owes it to the shareholders until the call is made. It is therefore a liability.\nNCERT shows it under Equity and Liabilities, in 'Current Liabilities', sub-head 'Other Current Liabilities'.\nIt is not added to paid-up capital, because that call has not yet been made.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-115","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":115,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"Under the Applications Supported by Blocked Amount (ASBA) process developed by SEBI, when is the applicant's bank account actually debited?","image":"","options":["Immediately on submitting the application, for the full application money","Only when securities are allotted, and only for the amount payable on the securities allotted","Only when the company makes the first call on the shares","On submitting the application, and the bank later refunds the excess to the applicant"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Under ASBA, the applicant authorises the bank to block (mark a lien on) the application money. The money is not debited at that stage.\nThe bank debits the account only after allotment, and only for the amount payable on the securities actually allotted.\nThe lien on the balance is then removed.\nOption (4) is wrong because no debit-and-refund takes place. The unused amount was only ever blocked.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-116","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":116,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"Case: Vibha Ltd. issued equity shares of Rs. 10 each at a premium of Rs. 3, payable as follows:\nApplication: Rs. 3\nAllotment: Rs. 5 (including premium of Rs. 3)\nFirst and final call: Rs. 5\nRaman, holding 400 shares, paid only the application money. His shares were forfeited after the first and final call. With what amount will Securities Premium Reserve Account be debited in the forfeiture entry?","image":"","options":["Nil","Rs. 2,000","Rs. 1,200","Rs. 3,200"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: premium was due with allotment, which Raman did not pay, so premium on his shares was never received.\nRule: when premium has not been received on forfeited shares, Securities Premium Reserve is debited with the unreceived premium.\nSubstituting: 400 × 3 = Rs. 1,200.\nEntry: Share Capital Dr. 4,000; Securities Premium Reserve Dr. 1,200; To Share Allotment 2,000; To Share First and Final Call 2,000; To Share Forfeiture 1,200.\n'Nil' would be correct only if the premium had been received.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-117","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":117,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"Case: Orion Ltd., a public company limited by shares, failed to pay Rs. 20 lakh to a supplier, Sagar Traders. Sagar Traders filed a suit against Meera, a shareholder who has fully paid for her 5,000 shares, and demanded the dues from her personal savings. Orion Ltd. holds land and a bank account in its own name.\nWhich of the following is the correct legal position?","image":"","options":["Meera is liable for the full Rs. 20 lakh because shareholders are the real owners of the company","Meera is liable up to the nominal value of her 5,000 shares, even though they are fully paid","Meera is not liable. The company is a separate legal entity that can be sued in its own name, and her liability ended once her shares were fully paid","Meera is liable only if the company is wound up, because her liability is limited by guarantee"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Separate legal entity: Orion Ltd. is distinct from its members. It holds property and a bank account in its own name and can sue or be sued in its own name. So Sagar Traders must proceed against the company.\nLimited liability: In a company limited by shares, a member who has paid the full amount of her shares has no further liability for the company's debts.\nOption (2) fails because she has already paid the full nominal value.\nOption (4) fails because the liability rule for guarantee companies does not apply to a company limited by shares.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-118","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":118,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"Shubh Ltd. purchased land from Verma Builders for Rs. 6,00,000. Shubh Ltd. paid the full price by issuing fully paid equity shares of Rs. 100 each at par. How many shares were issued to Verma Builders?","image":"","options":["60,000","5,000","6,000","6,600"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: Amount payable = Rs. 6,00,000; issue price = Rs. 100 (at par).\nFormula: Number of shares = Amount payable ÷ Issue price.\nSubstituting: 6,00,000 ÷ 100 = 6,000.\nSo, 6,000 shares are issued. 60,000 would assume a Rs. 10 share. 5,000 would assume a Rs. 120 issue price.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-119","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":119,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"On 1 April 2024, Sonia paid the final call of Rs. 4 per share on her 500 shares in advance. The final call was made and became due on 1 October 2024. The Articles are silent on interest, so the company pays interest at the maximum rate permitted by Table F. How much interest is payable to Sonia on calls in advance?","image":"","options":["Rs. 100","Rs. 240","Rs. 120","Rs. 200"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: calls in advance = 500 × Rs. 4 = Rs. 2,000; Table F maximum rate = 12% p.a.; period from 1 April to 1 October = 6 months.\nFormula: Interest = Amount × Rate × Time.\nSubstituting: 2,000 × 12/100 × 6/12 = Rs. 120.\nThe interest is debited to Interest on Calls in Advance A/c, an expense of the company.\nRs. 100 wrongly uses 10% p.a.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-120","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":120,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about forfeiture of shares are correct?\n(i) Share Capital A/c is debited with the called-up amount on the forfeited shares.\n(ii) Share Forfeiture A/c is credited with the amount received on the forfeited shares, excluding premium already received.\n(iii) Until the shares are reissued, the balance of Share Forfeiture A/c is shown under Reserves and Surplus.\n(iv) If a Calls in Arrears A/c is maintained, it is credited at the time of forfeiture.\n(v) Securities Premium Reserve A/c is debited on forfeiture even if the premium has been fully received.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: called-up capital is reversed.\n(ii) Correct: the forfeited amount excludes premium that was received.\n(iii) Incorrect: NCERT shows this balance as an addition to paid-up capital under 'Share Capital'.\n(iv) Correct: Calls in Arrears A/c replaces the individual call accounts.\n(v) Incorrect: premium already received is never reversed.\nCorrect statements = 3.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-121","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":121,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (ESOP terms) with List-II (meanings):\nList-I\n(a) Grant\n(b) Vesting period\n(c) Exercise\n(d) Exercise price\nList-II\n(i) Price payable by the employee for exercising the option\n(ii) Giving an option to employees to subscribe to shares at a pre-determined price\n(iii) Period between the grant date and the date by which all vesting conditions must be satisfied\n(iv) Applying by the employee for issue of shares against the option vested in him","image":"","options":["(a)-(ii), (b)-(iii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(i)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(iii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(i), (b)-(iii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) Grant means giving the option to subscribe at a pre-determined price → (ii).\n(b) Vesting period runs from grant date until all vesting conditions are satisfied → (iii).\n(c) Exercise means the employee applying for shares against the vested option → (iv).\n(d) Exercise price is the price the employee pays to exercise the option → (i).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-122","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":122,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"Under the Companies Act, a company ordinarily cannot issue shares at a discount. According to NCERT, in which of the following cases is an issue at a discount allowed?","image":"","options":["Issue of shares to vendors for purchase of assets, and issue of bonus shares","Reissue of forfeited shares, and issue of sweat equity shares","Issue of preference shares, and issue of shares through private placement","Issue of shares when the issue is undersubscribed, and issue of shares to promoters"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Key fact: As a general rule, a company cannot issue shares below their nominal value.\nNCERT names only two exceptions: reissue of forfeited shares and issue of sweat equity shares.\nShares issued to vendors may be at par or at a premium. Bonus shares are issued as fully paid. So option (1) is not an exception.\nPreference shares, private placement and undersubscription do not by themselves permit a discount.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-123","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":123,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about issue of shares for consideration other than cash is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["The number of shares issued depends on the issue price and the amount payable to the vendor.","Bank Account is debited when shares are allotted to the vendor.","For the same amount payable, a higher premium means fewer shares are issued to the vendor.","Shares allotted to vendors form part of the issued capital of the company."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: Number of shares = Amount payable ÷ Issue price.\n(2) Incorrect: No cash is received from the vendor. The vendor's account is debited, not Bank.\n(3) Correct: A higher issue price gives a smaller quotient, so fewer shares are issued.\n(4) Correct: NCERT defines issued capital to include shares allotted to vendors.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-124","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":124,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"Tara Ltd. has a subscribed capital of 1,50,000 equity shares of Rs. 10 each, of which Rs. 7 per share has been called up. Calls in arrears amount to Rs. 3,000. What is the uncalled capital of the company?","image":"","options":["Rs. 4,53,000","Rs. 4,50,000","Rs. 10,47,000","Rs. 10,50,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: face value Rs. 10, called up Rs. 7, so Rs. 3 per share is still uncalled.\nFormula: Uncalled capital = Subscribed shares × Uncalled amount per share.\nSubstituting: 1,50,000 × 3 = Rs. 4,50,000.\nCalls in arrears relate to capital already called, so they do not change the uncalled capital. Option (1) wrongly adds them.\nSo uncalled capital = Rs. 4,50,000.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-125","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":125,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"Case: Vardhan Ltd. invited applications for 50,000 equity shares of Rs. 10 each at a premium of Rs. 3 per share. The amount was payable as follows:\nRs. 4 on application (including Rs. 1 premium)\nRs. 5 on allotment (including Rs. 2 premium)\nthe balance on first and final call.\nApplications were received for 60,000 shares. Applications for 10,000 shares were rejected and their money refunded, and the remaining applicants were allotted shares in full. All amounts due were received.\nLater, the company used the Securities Premium Reserve to write off preliminary expenses of Rs. 40,000 and a discount of Rs. 15,000 allowed on issue of debentures.\nWhat is the balance of the Securities Premium Reserve shown under 'Reserves and Surplus' after these adjustments?","image":"","options":["Rs. 95,000","Rs. 1,05,000","Rs. 45,000","Rs. 1,10,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: 50,000 shares are allotted at a premium of Rs. 3 each (Rs. 1 with application and Rs. 2 with allotment).\nPremium on the 10,000 rejected applications is refunded, so it is never credited to the reserve.\nPremium credited = 50,000 × 3 = Rs. 1,50,000.\nPermitted write-offs = 40,000 + 15,000 = Rs. 55,000.\nBalance = 1,50,000 − 55,000 = Rs. 95,000.\nRs. 1,05,000 wrongly counts the premium on the rejected applications.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-126","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":126,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): When an issue of shares is over-subscribed, the subscribed capital shown in the books of the company is equal to the issued capital.\nReason (R): A company cannot allot more shares than the number offered for subscription, so the fact of over-subscription is not reflected in the books.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. If applications exceed the shares offered, the shares allotted equal the shares offered, so subscribed capital equals issued capital.\nR is true. NCERT states that when subscription is higher than the offer, the allotment equals the offer. The over-subscription therefore does not appear in the books.\nR is the reason subscribed capital stops at issued capital, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-127","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":127,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): When shares issued at a premium are forfeited for non-payment of allotment money that includes the premium, Securities Premium Reserve A/c is debited with the unpaid premium.\nReason (R): On forfeiture, the premium already received on the forfeited shares is transferred to Share Forfeiture A/c.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true. The premium was credited to Securities Premium Reserve when allotment became due. If that premium is never received, the credit must be cancelled on forfeiture, so the reserve is debited with the unpaid premium.\nR is false. Premium actually received is never reversed or transferred. It stays in Securities Premium Reserve, and Share Forfeiture A/c gets only the capital amount received.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-128","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":128,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"Neel Ltd. forfeited 300 equity shares of Rs. 10 each for non-payment of the first and final call of Rs. 4 per share. Rs. 6 per share had been received on these shares. All 300 shares were later reissued as fully paid at Rs. 7 per share. What amount will be transferred to Capital Reserve?","image":"","options":["Rs. 900","Rs. 1,800","Rs. 2,100","Rs. 1,200"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: 300 shares forfeited, Rs. 6 per share received, reissued at Rs. 7 as fully paid (face value Rs. 10).\nAmount forfeited = 300 × 6 = Rs. 1,800 (credited to Share Forfeiture A/c).\nDiscount on reissue = 300 × (10 − 7) = Rs. 900, debited to Share Forfeiture A/c. This is within the limit of Rs. 1,800.\nBalance on reissued shares = 1,800 − 900 = Rs. 900, transferred to Capital Reserve.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-129","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":129,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"Neel Ltd. allotted 50,000 equity shares of Rs. 10 each and called up Rs. 8 per share. A holder of 1,500 shares did not pay the first call of Rs. 2 per share. Another shareholder paid Rs. 5,000 as calls in advance. What amount of paid-up capital will appear in the Notes to Accounts under Share Capital?","image":"","options":["Rs. 3,97,000","Rs. 4,02,000","Rs. 4,00,000","Rs. 3,92,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: Called-up capital = 50,000 × 8 = Rs. 4,00,000.\nCalls in arrears = 1,500 × 2 = Rs. 3,000.\nFormula: Paid-up capital = Called-up capital − Calls in arrears.\nSubstituting: 4,00,000 − 3,000 = Rs. 3,97,000.\nCalls in advance (Rs. 5,000) is a current liability and is not added, so Rs. 4,02,000 is wrong.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-130","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":130,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Even when a share issue is heavily oversubscribed, the subscribed capital shown in the books does not exceed the issued capital.\nReason (R): When applications exceed the shares offered, the company allots only as many shares as it has offered.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: subscribed capital can be equal to or less than issued capital, never more. The fact of oversubscription is not reflected in the books.\nR is true: on oversubscription, allotment is limited to the number of shares offered.\nR is the very reason why subscribed capital cannot exceed issued capital, so it explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-131","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":131,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"Kiran Ltd. forfeited 300 equity shares of Rs. 10 each for non-payment of the final call of Rs. 3 per share. All the forfeited shares were reissued at Rs. 8 per share as fully paid. What amount will be transferred to Capital Reserve?","image":"","options":["Rs. 2,100","Rs. 1,500","Rs. 600","Rs. 900"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: amount forfeited = 300 × (10 − 3) = Rs. 2,100.\nReissue entry: Bank A/c Dr. 2,400 and Share Forfeiture A/c Dr. 600 (discount of Rs. 2 × 300), To Share Capital A/c 3,000.\nBalance in the forfeiture account = 2,100 − 600 = Rs. 1,500.\nAll shares have been reissued, so the whole Rs. 1,500 goes to Capital Reserve.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-132","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":132,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"Kiran Ltd. bought land from Mehta & Sons. The price is to be paid by issuing shares of the company. Which journal entry records the purchase of the land, before the shares are issued?","image":"","options":["Mehta & Sons Dr.\nTo Land A/c","Land A/c Dr.\nTo Mehta & Sons","Land A/c Dr.\nTo Bank A/c","Land A/c Dr.\nTo Share Application A/c"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The asset acquired is debited, and the vendor is credited as a creditor of the company.\nSo the entry is Land A/c Dr. To Mehta & Sons, like the Building A/c Dr. To Handa Ltd. entry in NCERT.\nOption (1) reverses the entry. Bank is not credited because no cash is paid. No share application money is received from a vendor.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-133","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":133,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"Arjun Ltd. forfeited 500 equity shares of Rs. 10 each for non-payment of the final call of Rs. 4 per share. All earlier amounts had been received. What is the maximum discount per share at which these shares can be reissued as fully paid?","image":"","options":["Rs. 4","Rs. 6","Rs. 10","Rs. 2"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: face value Rs. 10 and unpaid final call Rs. 4, so the amount received per share was Rs. 10 − Rs. 4 = Rs. 6.\nRule: the maximum discount on reissue equals the amount forfeited per share.\nSo the maximum discount is Rs. 6 per share, which means the minimum reissue price is Rs. 4.\nRs. 4 is the tempting option, but it is the unpaid call, not the amount forfeited.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-134","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":134,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"Vardhan Ltd. offered 15,000 equity shares of Rs. 10 each at par, payable Rs. 3 on application. Applications were received for 18,000 shares. The directors rejected the applications for 3,000 shares, refunded their money in full, and allotted shares to the remaining applicants. What amount will be credited to Equity Share Capital A/c when the application money is transferred?","image":"","options":["Rs. 54,000","Rs. 45,000","Rs. 9,000","Rs. 36,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: 18,000 shares applied for, 15,000 shares allotted, application money Rs. 3 per share.\nApplication money received = 18,000 × 3 = Rs. 54,000 (Bank Dr., Share Application Cr.).\nRefund on rejected applications = 3,000 × 3 = Rs. 9,000 (Share Application Dr., Bank Cr.).\nOnly the money on allotted shares goes to capital: 15,000 × 3 = Rs. 45,000.\nRs. 54,000 is wrong because it includes the money on the rejected applications.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-135","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":135,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The balance of Calls in Advance Account is not added to the paid-up capital of a company.\nReason (R): Calls in Advance is a liability of the company until the related call becomes due.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. The balance of Calls in Advance appears under 'Other Current Liabilities' in the balance sheet. It is not shown with paid-up capital.\nR is true. Money received before a call is made is owed by the company to the shareholder until it is adjusted against that call.\nR explains A. The amount is excluded from paid-up capital precisely because it is a liability.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-136","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":136,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The balance of Securities Premium Reserve can be used to write off the preliminary expenses of the company.\nStatement II: Securities Premium Reserve is shown under the head 'Share Capital' in the balance sheet.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Writing off preliminary expenses is one of the five permitted uses of securities premium.\nStatement II is incorrect. Securities premium is shown under 'Reserves and Surplus' within Shareholders' Funds. It is not part of Share Capital.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-137","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":137,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"Arya Ltd. offered 20,000 equity shares of Rs. 10 each, payable Rs. 3 on application and Rs. 4 on allotment. Applications were received for 30,000 shares. Applications for 5,000 shares were rejected and their money refunded. The remaining applicants received shares pro-rata, and excess application money was adjusted towards allotment. Ramesh, who was allotted 800 shares, failed to pay the allotment money. What is the amount unpaid by Ramesh on allotment?","image":"","options":["Rs. 3,200","Rs. 2,000","Rs. 2,600","Rs. 3,800"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: 20,000 shares were allotted to applicants for 25,000 shares, a ratio of 4 : 5.\nStep 1: Ramesh applied for 800 × 5/4 = 1,000 shares.\nStep 2: Excess application money = (1,000 − 800) × 3 = Rs. 600.\nStep 3: Allotment due = 800 × 4 = Rs. 3,200.\nStep 4: Unpaid on allotment = 3,200 − 600 = Rs. 2,600.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-138","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":138,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The amount received as calls in advance is not added to the paid-up capital of the company.\nReason (R): Calls in advance is a liability of the company until the related call is made, and it is later adjusted against that call when it falls due.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT states that the calls in advance balance is shown separately under Other Current Liabilities and is not added to paid-up capital.\nR is true. The money belongs to a call not yet made, so the company owes it to the shareholder. On the due date it is adjusted by: Calls in Advance A/c Dr., To Particular Call A/c.\nBecause it is a liability and not yet capital, R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-139","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":139,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II:\nList-I\n(A) Discount allowed on reissue of forfeited shares\n(B) Premium received on reissue of forfeited shares\n(C) Surplus on forfeited shares that have been reissued\n(D) Forfeiture balance on shares not yet reissued\nList-II\n(i) Credited to Securities Premium Reserve\n(ii) Added to paid-up capital under Share Capital\n(iii) Debited to Share Forfeiture Account\n(iv) Transferred to Capital Reserve","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(i), C-(iv), D-(ii)","A-(iv), B-(i), C-(iii), D-(ii)","A-(iii), B-(ii), C-(iv), D-(i)","A-(i), B-(iii), C-(ii), D-(iv)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) The discount on reissue is debited to Share Forfeiture A/c → (iii).\n(B) The premium on reissue is credited to Securities Premium Reserve → (i).\n(C) The surplus on reissued shares is a capital profit and goes to Capital Reserve → (iv).\n(D) The balance relating to shares not yet reissued is shown as an addition to paid-up capital under Share Capital → (ii).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-140","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":140,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): A company may use its Securities Premium Reserve to issue fully paid bonus shares to its members.\nReason (R): Securities Premium Reserve is shown under the head 'Reserves and Surplus' in the balance sheet.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A is true: issuing fully paid bonus shares is one of the five permitted uses of securities premium.\nR is true: the balance is shown under Reserves and Surplus.\nHowever, R does not explain A. The use for bonus shares is allowed because the Act specifically permits it, not because of where the balance appears in the balance sheet.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-141","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":141,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"As per Section 42 of the Companies Act, 2013, 'Private Placement' means:","image":"","options":["An offer of securities to the general public through a prospectus","An offer of securities or an invitation to subscribe securities to a select group of persons through a private placement offer letter","An option given to employees to buy shares at a price lower than the market price","Issue of fully paid shares to vendors for assets purchased"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Section 42 describes private placement as an offer of securities, or an invitation to subscribe to them, made to a select group of persons through a private placement offer letter.\nOption (1) describes a public issue made through a prospectus.\nOption (3) describes ESOP.\nOption (4) describes issue of shares for consideration other than cash.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-142","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":142,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"The finance team of a company proposes the following uses of its Securities Premium Reserve balance:\n(i) Issuing fully paid bonus shares, not exceeding its unissued share capital\n(ii) Writing off preliminary expenses\n(iii) Paying dividend to equity shareholders\n(iv) Paying premium on redemption of debentures\n(v) Buying back its own shares\n(vi) Writing off discount allowed on issue of debentures\nHow many of the above are permitted uses of the Securities Premium Reserve?","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Permitted: issue of fully paid bonus shares.\n(ii) Permitted: writing off preliminary expenses.\n(iii) Not permitted: dividend is not among the five purposes. Securities premium is a capital receipt, not a distributable profit.\n(iv) Permitted: premium on redemption of preference shares or debentures.\n(v) Permitted: buy-back of the company's own shares.\n(vi) Permitted: writing off expenses, commission or discount on issue of securities.\nTotal permitted = 5.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-143","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":143,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"Vikas Ltd. issued 8,000 shares of Rs. 100 each, payable as Rs. 25 on application, Rs. 35 on allotment, Rs. 25 on first call and the balance on second and final call. All shares were allotted. All money was received except the second and final call on 200 shares. What amount is debited to Bank A/c on receipt of the second and final call?","image":"","options":["Rs. 1,20,000","Rs. 1,17,000","Rs. 1,95,000","Rs. 1,15,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: Second and final call = 100 − (25 + 35 + 25) = Rs. 15 per share.\nAmount due = 8,000 × 15 = Rs. 1,20,000.\nUnpaid amount = 200 × 15 = Rs. 3,000.\nSo Bank A/c is debited with 1,20,000 − 3,000 = Rs. 1,17,000.\nOption (1) ignores the unpaid call.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-144","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":144,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"Rakesh held 40% of the shares of Sunlite Fabrics Ltd. After his sudden death, his shares passed to his legal heirs. The company went on with its business without any break, and its existing contracts stayed valid.\nWhich feature of a company is shown in this case?","image":"","options":["Separate legal entity","Perpetual succession","Limited liability","Common seal"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Key fact: A company is an artificial person created by law. It continues to exist whatever changes happen in its membership. Only law can terminate it.\nThe death, insanity or insolvency of a member does not affect the company's existence. This feature is called perpetual succession.\nWhy (1) is wrong: separate legal entity means the company is distinct from its members and can own property and make contracts in its own name. That idea is related, but the continuity of the business despite a member's death is specifically perpetual succession.\nOptions (3) and (4) deal with members' liability and the company's official signature, so they do not fit.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-145","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":145,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: When an issue is oversubscribed, the subscribed capital shown in the books equals the number of shares offered, not the number applied for.\nStatement II: In a pro-rata allotment, if the excess application money exceeds the amount due on allotment, the surplus may either be refunded or credited to Calls in Advance Account.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. NCERT states that when applications exceed the offer, allotment equals the offer. The fact of oversubscription is not reflected in the books.\nStatement II is correct. Under the second alternative, excess money beyond the allotment due may be refunded or credited to calls in advance. Janta Papers' third case shows the Calls-in-Advance treatment.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-146","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":146,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about preference shares, as described under Section 43, are correct?\n(i) They carry a preferential right to dividend before any dividend is paid to equity shareholders.\n(ii) Their dividend may be a fixed amount or an amount calculated at a fixed rate on the nominal value of each share.\n(iii) On winding up, they carry a preferential right to repayment of capital before equity shareholders.\n(iv) Preference shareholders can never participate in the surplus profits of the company.\n(v) Their dividend is calculated at a fixed rate on the market value of the shares.","image":"","options":["2","4","3","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: they have a preferential right to dividend over equity shares.\n(ii) Correct: the dividend is either a fixed amount or a fixed rate on the nominal value.\n(iii) Correct: they have a preferential right to repayment of capital on winding up.\n(iv) Incorrect: participating preference shares may share in surpluses, as specified in the Memorandum or Articles.\n(v) Incorrect: the rate is applied to the nominal value, not the market value.\nSo 3 statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-147","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":147,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"Shares are issued to a vendor at a premium as payment for an asset. In the entry discharging the vendor, which account is credited besides Share Capital Account?","image":"","options":["Capital Reserve Account","Securities Premium Reserve Account","General Reserve Account","Share Forfeiture Account"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The entry is: Vendor A/c Dr. To Share Capital A/c (face value) To Securities Premium Reserve A/c (premium).\nAny premium on an issue of shares goes to Securities Premium Reserve, whether the shares are issued for cash or not.\nCapital Reserve receives profit on reissue of forfeited shares, not premium.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-148","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":148,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The fact that an issue was over-subscribed does not appear in the share capital shown in the books of the company.\nReason (R): When applications exceed the shares offered, the company allots only the number of shares it offered.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. Subscribed capital can be equal to or less than issued capital, but it can never be more. So over-subscription is not reflected in the books.\nR is true. When applications exceed the offer, allotment is made equal to the offer.\nBecause allotment is capped at the number offered, share capital never shows the extra applications. R therefore correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-149","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":149,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"Kavya Ltd. issued shares of Rs. 10 each at Rs. 12 per share. The premium of Rs. 2 was received with allotment. Arjun holds 500 shares. He paid everything except the first and final call of Rs. 3 per share, and his shares were forfeited. What amount will be credited to Share Forfeiture Account?","image":"","options":["Rs. 3,500","Rs. 4,500","Rs. 5,000","Rs. 1,500"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: face value Rs. 10, premium Rs. 2 (already received), unpaid call Rs. 3 on 500 shares.\nThe premium has been received, so it is not reversed and is excluded from the forfeited amount.\nAmount paid towards capital = Rs. 10 - Rs. 3 = Rs. 7 per share.\nForfeiture credit = 500 x 7 = Rs. 3,500.\nThe entry is: Share Capital Dr. 5,000; To First and Final Call 1,500; To Share Forfeiture 3,500.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-150","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":150,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"Riya held 400 equity shares of Rs. 10 each issued at Rs. 12. The shares were payable as Rs. 3 on application, Rs. 5 on allotment (including premium of Rs. 2) and Rs. 4 on first and final call. She paid only the application money, and her shares were forfeited after the final call. With what amount will the Share Forfeiture Account be credited?","image":"","options":["Rs. 1,200","Rs. 2,000","Rs. 2,800","Rs. 4,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: Riya paid only application money = 400 × 3 = Rs. 1,200.\nDebits: Share Capital 400 × 10 = Rs. 4,000. Securities Premium Reserve 400 × 2 = Rs. 800, because the premium was not received.\nCredits: Allotment 400 × 5 = Rs. 2,000. Call 400 × 4 = Rs. 1,600. Share Forfeiture = amount actually received = Rs. 1,200.\nCheck: 4,800 = 2,000 + 1,600 + 1,200.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-151","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":151,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II:\nList-I (Type of share)\nA. Participating preference shares\nB. Cumulative preference shares\nC. Redeemable preference shares\nD. Equity shares with differential rights\nList-II (Feature)\n(i) Capital is repaid by the company after a stated period\n(ii) Unpaid dividend of a year is carried forward and paid out of later profits\n(iii) Carry voting or dividend rights that differ from other equity shares, as per rules and Articles\n(iv) Also share in surplus profits beyond the fixed dividend\nChoose the correct option:","image":"","options":["A-(iv), B-(ii), C-(i), D-(iii)","A-(ii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(iii)","A-(iv), B-(ii), C-(iii), D-(i)","A-(i), B-(ii), C-(iv), D-(iii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A-(iv): Participating preference shareholders may share in the company's surpluses, fully or to a limited extent, over and above the fixed dividend.\nB-(ii): For cumulative preference shares, arrears of dividend accumulate and are paid out of future profits.\nC-(i): Redeemable preference shares are repaid by the company after a stated period.\nD-(iii): Equity shares may carry differential rights as to voting, dividend or otherwise.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-152","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":152,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"Arjun Ltd. purchased machinery from Vega Ltd. for Rs. 4,50,000. It paid Rs. 30,000 by cheque and the balance by issuing equity shares of Rs. 10 each at a premium of 20%. How many shares will be issued to Vega Ltd.?","image":"","options":["35,000 shares","42,000 shares","37,500 shares","45,000 shares"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: Amount payable in shares = 4,50,000 − 30,000 = Rs. 4,20,000.\nIssue price = 10 + 20% of 10 = Rs. 12.\nFormula: Number of shares = Amount payable ÷ Issue price.\nSubstituting: 4,20,000 ÷ 12 = 35,000.\nSo 35,000 shares will be issued. Option 2 (42,000) wrongly divides by the face value of Rs. 10 instead of the issue price.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-153","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":153,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"Arjun Ltd. purchased a building from Lotus Ltd. It paid 20% of the price in cash. The balance was paid by issuing 9,600 equity shares of Rs. 50 each at a premium of 25%. What was the purchase price of the building?","image":"","options":["Rs. 7,50,000","Rs. 7,20,000","Rs. 6,00,000","Rs. 5,76,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: 9,600 shares; face value Rs. 50; premium 25% = Rs. 12.50.\nIssue price = 50 + 12.50 = Rs. 62.50.\nValue of shares issued = 9,600 × 62.50 = Rs. 6,00,000.\nThe shares cover 80% of the price, so Price = 6,00,000 ÷ 0.80 = Rs. 7,50,000.\nRs. 7,20,000 wrongly adds 20% to the value of the shares instead of dividing by 0.80.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-154","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":154,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"Sitara Ltd. offered 50,000 equity shares of Rs. 10 each at par, payable as follows: Rs. 3 on application, Rs. 4 on allotment and Rs. 3 on first and final call. Applications were received for 60,000 shares, and a pro-rata allotment was made to all applicants. Excess application money was adjusted towards allotment. Rani was allotted 1,000 shares. She failed to pay the allotment and call money, and her shares were forfeited. What amount will be credited to Share Forfeiture Account?","image":"","options":["Rs. 3,000","Rs. 3,600","Rs. 4,200","Rs. 600"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: pro-rata ratio 50,000 : 60,000 = 5 : 6. So Rani applied for 1,000 x 6/5 = 1,200 shares.\nApplication money paid = 1,200 x 3 = Rs. 3,600. Of this, Rs. 3,000 went to capital and Rs. 600 was adjusted against allotment.\nThe entry is: Share Capital Dr. 10,000; To Allotment (4,000 - 600) 3,400; To Call 3,000; To Share Forfeiture 3,600.\nSo the forfeited amount = Rs. 3,600, which is all the money she paid.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-155","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":155,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are permissible uses of the Securities Premium Reserve as per the chapter?\n(i) Issuing fully paid bonus shares\n(ii) Writing off preliminary expenses\n(iii) Paying dividend to equity shareholders\n(iv) Paying premium on redemption of debentures\n(v) Buy-back of its own shares\n(vi) Writing off loss on sale of machinery","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Permitted: issuing fully paid bonus shares, up to the unissued capital.\n(ii) Permitted: writing off preliminary expenses.\n(iii) Not permitted: the premium cannot be distributed as dividend.\n(iv) Permitted: paying premium on redemption of preference shares or debentures.\n(v) Permitted: buy-back of shares.\n(vi) Not permitted: a trading or asset loss is not among the five purposes.\nPermissible uses = 4.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-156","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":156,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"According to Section 43 of the Companies Act, 2013, an equity share is:","image":"","options":["A share that carries a fixed rate of dividend","A share that is redeemable after a fixed period","A share that is not a preference share","A share that carries a preferential right to repayment of capital"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Section 43 defines an equity share negatively: it is a share which is not a preference share.\nIn other words, it enjoys no preferential right either in the payment of dividend or in the repayment of capital.\nA fixed dividend and a preferential right to repayment of capital are features of preference shares.\nRedeemability is one of the ways preference shares are classified.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-157","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":157,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"Siya Ltd. is issuing equity shares of face value Rs. 10 each to the public. What is the minimum amount per share it must collect as application money?","image":"","options":["Rs. 0.50","Rs. 1.00","Rs. 2.50","Rs. 0.25"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: face value = Rs. 10 per share.\nRule: application money should be at least 5% of the face value of the share.\nSubstituting: 5% × Rs. 10 = Rs. 0.50.\nRs. 2.50 is 25% of face value. That figure is the maximum for a call, not the minimum application money.\nSo the minimum application money = Rs. 0.50 per share.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-SHARE-CAPITAL-158","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Share Capital","qno":158,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Share Capital","type":"mcq","question":"Kiran Ltd. has 10,000, 10% cumulative preference shares of Rs. 100 each and 1,00,000 equity shares of Rs. 10 each, all fully paid. No dividend was paid in 2023-24 due to inadequate profits. For 2024-25, profits of Rs. 3,50,000 are to be fully distributed. What is the dividend per equity share for 2024-25?","image":"","options":["Rs. 2.50","Rs. 3.50","Rs. 1.50","Rs. 1.00"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: Preference capital = 10,000 × Rs. 100 = Rs. 10,00,000 at 10%, cumulative. Dividend for 2023-24 is in arrears. Profit = Rs. 3,50,000.\nFormula: Equity dividend = Profit − (Arrears + Current preference dividend).\nSubstituting: Annual preference dividend = Rs. 1,00,000. For two years = Rs. 2,00,000. Balance = Rs. 3,50,000 − Rs. 2,00,000 = Rs. 1,50,000.\nPer equity share = Rs. 1,50,000 ÷ 1,00,000 = Rs. 1.50.\nRs. 2.50 would result only if the shares were non-cumulative, so that the arrears lapse.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Share Capital","difficulty":"hard"}]});
